window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[2]={"pages":[{"pdf":38,"printed":23,"ch":2,"text":"CHAPTER 2\n\nLayer 1: Climate Science and Scenarios\nHow to use these notes, and a map of Layer 1\nThese notes are written so you can work from them and skip the originals for concepts and headline numbers. Each section follows the same pattern: a source line, vocabulary, the argument or findings, a quotables table (quote, page, where to use it), what it means for your thesis, and a verdict on whether you can skip the original. Where the verdict says to open a specific page, do that before quoting it. Two rules. First, check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis; I read from extracted text and the page numbers are the documents' own printed numbers. Second, where I give my own inference rather than the source's claim, the text says so.\n\nBefore you start: what you need to know first\nLayer 1 has almost no prerequisites. You need four climate ideas, and each is defined again in the section where it first matters:\n• Emissions, concentration, forcing and temperature are four different things. Emissions are flows (GtCO2 a year); concentration is the stock in the air (ppm); radiative forcing is the energy imbalance that stock causes (W/m²); temperature is the response. The RCP names (2.6 to 8.5) are about forcing.\n• CO2-equivalent (CO2e) puts all greenhouse gases on one scale, so emission totals can be compared.\n• A cumulative budget exists because warming follows total CO2 emitted, not the yearly rate. This is why ”net zero” dates matter.\n• A scenario is a conditional story, not a forecast. If you remember one thing, remember this. Nothing in Layer 1 requires finance or economics. What Layer 1 does not give you is any link to banks; that comes from Layers 3 to 6.\n\nThe map\nTable 2.1. How to use these notes, and a map of Layer 1: The map\nSection\n\nSource\n\nOne-line job\n\nRCP paper (2011)\n\nThe physical scenarios: 2.6, 4.5, 6, 8.5 W/m².\n\nSSP papers (2014 and\n2017)\n\nFive development pathways with no climate policy; the policy\naxis comes separately."},{"pdf":39,"printed":24,"ch":2,"text":"Section\n\nSource\n\nOne-line job\n\nIPCC AR6 Synthesis\nReport (2023)\n\nThe combined message: warming levels, carbon budgets, net\nzero timing, stranded assets.\n\nIPCC AR6 WG3 SPM\n(2022)\n\nThe transition side: cuts required, fossil fuel decline,\nstranded-asset timing, finance.\n\nIPCC AR6 WG2\n(2022)\n\nThe physical risk side: hazard, exposure, vulnerability; Asia\nand India.\n\nMaster list\n\nThe ten best quotes and how it all feeds the thesis.\n\nReading the scenario labels\nA label like SSP1-2.6 joins two ideas: the development pathway (SSP1) and the forcing level in W/m² reached by 2100 (2.6, from the RCP paper). IPCC also groups pathways by warming level and category (C1 to C8) in the WG3 and Synthesis Report tables. Sections 1 and 2 explain the pieces; section 3 shows how AR6 uses them.\n\nIf you only have time for part of it\n1. Read section 6 first (the ten quotes and the four ideas).\n2. Then section 4, the strongest source for the stranded-asset case.\n3. Then section 5 for the physical risk framework.\n4. Sections 1 to 3 when you write the scenario chapter.\n\n1. van Vuuren et al. (2011), The Representative Concentration\nPathways\nSource: van Vuuren, Edmonds, Kainuma, Riahi, Thomson, Hibbard, Hurtt, Kram, Krey, Lamarque, Masui, Meinshausen, Nakicenovic, Smith, Rose (2011), Climatic Change 109:5–31 (your file ”RCp.pdf”, 27 pages). Page numbers below are the journal's own. The paper explains where the four RCPs (RCP2.6, 4.5, 6 and 8.5) come from and what they are, and just as importantly what they are not. An RCP fixes how much extra heat the atmosphere traps by 2100 and does not describe a whole future world.\n\nWhy this paper exists\nClimate models need a common set of future inputs, so that results from different labs can be compared. Older sets (IS92, SRES) did not give enough detail for modern climate models and did not include climate policy. The IPCC asked the research community to build a new set (p. 6–7). The community chose a three-phase plan (p. 7):\n1. Build four pathways of emissions, concentrations and land use (the RCPs).\n2. In parallel, run climate models on them and write new socio-economic scenarios (this became the SSPs, next section).\n3. Integrate and disseminate."},{"pdf":40,"printed":25,"ch":2,"text":"The RCPs were deliberately built first so that climate modellers did not have to wait for the economists.\n\nTechnical terms in this paper\nTable 2.2. van Vuuren et al. (2011): Technical terms in this paper\nTerm\n\nPlain meaning\n\nRadiative forcing (W/m²) Extra energy the Earth traps per square metre, relative to pre-industrial times. Higher means more warming. This is the number in each RCP's name. Concentration pathway\n\nA path of greenhouse-gas concentrations in the air over time, used as\ninput to a climate model. The paper stresses it is an input, not a full\nscenario (p. 7).\n\nIAM (integrated\nassessment model)\n\nAn economy–energy–land–climate model used to ask how emissions would\nevolve under different technology and policy assumptions. Four IAM\nteams built the four RCPs.\n\nHarmonization\n\nAdjusting each model's output so that its starting year (2000 or 2005)\nmatches observed historical data, then blending back to the model's own\npath (p. 14).\n\nDownscaling\n\nSpreading regional model output onto a 0.5° × 0.5° grid, so climate\nmodels can use it (p. 13–14).\n\nECP (Extended\nConcentration Pathway)\n\nA simple, stylised extension of each RCP from 2100 to 2300, not built\nwith IAMs (p. 15).\n\nKaya identity\n\nEmissions = population × income per person × energy per unit of income\n× emissions per unit of energy. Used to see what drives a scenario (p. 17).\n\nOvershoot\n\nConcentrations rise above the target and are later brought back down.\n\nBECCS\n\nBioenergy with carbon capture and storage, which removes CO2 from the\nair ('negative emissions'). RCP2.6 depends on it (p. 17).\n\nThe four pathways\nTable 2.3. van Vuuren et al. (2011): The four pathways\nPathway 2100 forcing\n\nShape (p. 12)\n\nIAM team\n\nNotes\n\nRCP2.6 2.6 W/m² (about\n490 ppm CO2-eq\nat peak)\n\nPeaks near 3 W/m²\nmid-century, then\ndeclines\n\nIMAGE\n(PBL,\nNetherlands)\n\nNeeds strong policy and negative\nemissions from bioenergy plus CCS\n(p. 17)\n\nRCP4.5 4.5 W/m² (about\n650 ppm CO2-eq)\n\nStabilises after 2100, GCAM\nno overshoot\n(PNNL,\nUSA)\n\nAssumes carbon in natural\nvegetation is valued, so forests\nexpand (p. 20)\n\nRCP6\n\nStabilises after 2100, AIM\nno overshoot\n(NIES,\nJapan)\n\nHeavy fossil reliance, constant\ncarbon factor (p. 18)\n\n6 W/m² (about\n850 ppm CO2-eq)\n\nRCP8.5 8.5 W/m² (about Keeps rising through MESSAGE Based on a revised SRES A2 1370 ppm CO2-eq) 2100 (IIASA, storyline: high population, lower Austria) incomes, slow technology (p. 17)"},{"pdf":41,"printed":26,"ch":2,"text":"How they were chosen (p. 10–11): the team reviewed 324 scenarios from the literature, of which 37 (from 7 modelling teams) met the criteria. They wanted four, an even number so there is no 'middle' scenario, spaced about 2 W/m² apart. The set had to cover the literature range, from about 2.5 to 8–9 W/m². RCP8.5 sits near the 90th percentile of no-policy baseline scenarios; RCP4.5 matches the bulk of the scenarios assessed in the IPCC's Fourth Assessment Report.\n\nWhat the paper finds about the pathways (section 3)\n• Energy. RCP2.6, 4.5 and 6 use 750–900 EJ of primary energy in 2100, about double today. RCP8.5 is far more energy-intensive because of population growth and slow technology (p. 17).\n• Fossil fuels. Even in the low scenarios, carbon capture means some coal or gas is still used in 2100 at higher levels than in 2000 (p. 17).\n• Land. RCP8.5 expands cropland and grassland; RCP2.6 expands cropland for bioenergy; RCP4.5 shrinks both through reforestation (p. 19–20).\n• Co-benefits. Climate policy also cuts air pollutants such as SO2 because both come from burning fossil fuels, especially coal (p. 21). All four RCPs assume air-pollution control tightens as income rises, so they are not suitable for studying worlds where it does not (p. 22).\n• Methane versus nitrous oxide. Methane breaks down faster, so cuts show up in concentrations sooner; N2O lasts longer, so its concentration rises in every RCP (p. 23).\n\nThe limitations section is the most useful part (p. 26–28)\n1. RCPs are not forecasts, not absolute bounds, and not policy recommendations. No likelihood is attached to any of them.\n2. The socio-economic stories behind the four RCPs were built independently by four teams, so the set has no common internal logic. Differences between RCPs can come from model differences, not only from climate policy.\n3. The socio-economic scenario behind each RCP is one of many that could produce the same concentrations.\n4. Land-use and air-pollution patterns reflect each model's own assumptions, not only the forcing level.\n5. Turning emissions into concentrations and forcing carries its own uncertainty. The paper's own conclusion is that a socio-economic framework still had to be built to go with the RCPs. That is the next section.\n\nQuotables"},{"pdf":42,"printed":27,"ch":2,"text":"Table 2.4. van Vuuren et al. (2011): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\n”The word ‘representative’ signifies that each of\nthe RCPs represents a larger set of scenarios in\nthe literature.”\n\nDefining what an RCP is in your\nscenario chapter\n\nRCPs ”are not the final new, fully integrated\nscenarios”\n\nStating that RCPs cover climate forcing\nonly, not economics or policy\n\n”The RCPs should not be interpreted as forecasts\nor absolute bounds, or be seen as policy\nprescriptive.”\n\nA caveat whenever you use an\nRCP-based scenario\n\n”The socio-economic scenarios underlying the\nRCPs cannot be treated as a set with an\noverarching internal logic.”\n\nWhy RCPs alone cannot drive a\ntransition-risk analysis\n\n”Differences between the RCPs, therefore, cannot 27\nbe directly interpreted as a result of climate policy\nor particular socio-economic developments.”\n\nWarning against reading policy\nconclusions off RCP comparisons\n\nRCPs span ”2.6 to 8.5 W/m2” of year-2100\nradiative forcing\n\nOne-line summary of the range\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• Physical versus transition. The RCPs describe the physical side: how much warming results from a given amount of gas. They say nothing about how an economy or a bank behaves. Transition risk needs the policy and technology layer that comes with the SSPs and with the scenario sets built on both. (That link to the scenarios used by central banks is my background knowledge, not something this paper says.)\n• Why low scenarios imply transition risk. RCP2.6 shows what a low-warming world demands: peak and fast decline in emissions, plus negative emissions. That is the physical logic behind the idea that fossil-fuel assets may be stranded.\n• Reading the labels. When a report says ”SSP1-2.6”, the 2.6 is this paper's forcing level, in W/m². Can you skip the original? Mostly yes. If you need the exact harmonization method or the data tables (Tables 1–3), open the paper; for citation and concepts, these notes are enough. Check any quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n2. The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers)\nSources: (a) O'Neill, Kriegler, Riahi, Ebi, Hallegatte, Carter, Mathur, van Vuuren (2014), Climatic Change 122:387–400, ”A new scenario framework for climate change research: the concept of shared socioeconomic pathways” (your file ”SSP-1.pdf”, 14 pages). (b) O'Neill, Kriegler, Ebi, Kemp-Benedict, Riahi, Rothman, van Ruijven, van Vuuren, Birkmann, Kok, Levy, Solecki (2017), Global Environmental Change 42:169–180, ”The roads ahead: Narratives for shared socioeconomic pathways describing world futures in the 21st century” (your file ”SSP-2.pdf”, 12 pages; first published online in 2015). Page numbers are the journals' own. Ritu Mathur of TERI, New Delhi, is a co-author of the 2014 paper."},{"pdf":43,"printed":28,"ch":2,"text":"The RCPs say how much warming; the SSPs say what kind of society is producing and facing it. An SSP describes a possible world through 2100 in which there is no climate change and no new climate policy, so that researchers can add those later and see their effects separately.\n\nPaper (a), the framework: how to think about the SSPs\n1. The scenario matrix (p.\ningredients:\n\n388). Integrated climate scenarios are built from three\n\nTable 2.5. The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers): Paper (a), the framework: how to think about the SSPs Axis\n\nWhat it fixes\n\nWhere it comes from\n\nClimate outcome\n\nHow much radiative forcing and warming\n\nThe four RCPs\n\nSocio-economic\ndevelopment\n\nPopulation, income, technology, institutions, in a\nworld without climate policy or climate change\n\nThe SSPs\n\nPolicy\n\nWhat climate policy is assumed, to reach a given\nclimate outcome\n\nShared climate Policy\nAssumptions (SPAs)\n\nThe SSPs are only the middle axis. A full scenario needs all three.\n2. SSPs are deliberately unrealistic in one respect (p. 389). They assume no climate change and no new climate policy. The authors accept that this means they ”may not describe plausible assumptions for the future, but this is an intentional component of the design.” Reason: if the SSP already contains climate effects, a researcher cannot isolate the effect of climate or of policy.\n3. Pathways versus scenarios (p. 389–390). A pathway is one component (an RCP or an SSP). A scenario combines pathways with emissions, climate projections and policy to give an integrated picture. Emissions and land use are outputs of models run on the SSP, not part of the SSP.\n4. The challenges space (p. 390–391). Instead of choosing two causes and building a 2 × 2 of worlds, as the older SRES scenarios did, the authors begin with outcomes. They plot worlds by two kinds of difficulty: how hard it is to mitigate (cut emissions) and how hard it is to adapt. This is called the inverse approach.\n• Challenges to mitigation (p. 391): high reference emissions without policy (fast population or economic growth, energy-intensive economies, carbon-intensive energy supply), and low mitigative capacity (few technology options, weak institutions, little finance, little political will).\n• Challenges to adaptation (p. 392): conditions that raise the risk from any given climate change. Risk is the combination of hazard, exposure and vulnerability. The hazard part sits in the RCP and climate models. Exposure, sensitivity and adaptive capacity sit in the SSP. (Compare the IPCC WG2 risk framework later in this layer.)\n5. Five domains, one pathway each (p. 393). The challenges space is split into five domains, and one SSP is chosen to represent each. Many pathways could fall in each domain; the SSP is the one picked to be shared across studies."},{"pdf":44,"printed":29,"ch":2,"text":"6. Basic and extended SSPs (p. 394–397). Basic SSPs have a narrative and a few quantified drivers (population, GDP, urbanisation). Extended SSPs add sector or regional detail (for example energy, land, agriculture) as needed.\n7. Drivers listed in Table 1 (p. 396): demographics, economic development, welfare, environment, resources, institutions and governance, technology, broader societal factors, and non-climate policies.\n\nPaper (b), the narratives: what each SSP says\nThe 2017 paper gives the stories. They were written by expert judgment through a series of meetings and a public comment round (38 pages of comments from 18 reviewers, p. 172). Table 2.6. The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers): Paper (b), the narratives: what each SSP says Challenge Challenge to adaptamitigation tion In one line\n\nSSP\n\nName\n\nSSP1\n\nSustainability: taking\nthe green road\n\nLow\n\nLow\n\nInclusive, lower-resource development; low\npopulation; rapid shift to renewables\n\nSSP2\n\nMiddle of the road\n\nModerate\n\nModerate\n\nTrends continue from history; uneven\nprogress; slow fall in fossil dependence\n\nSSP3\n\nRegional rivalry: a\nrocky road\n\nHigh\n\nHigh\n\nNationalism, weak institutions, slow growth,\nhigh inequality, fossil dependence\n\nSSP4\n\nInequality: a road\ndivided\n\nLow\n\nHigh\n\nA connected, high-tech elite and a poor,\nisolated majority; climate action is easy for\nthe few, adaptation hard for the many\n\nSSP5\n\nFossil-fueled\ndevelopment: taking\nthe highway\n\nHigh\n\nLow\n\nRapid growth driven by coal, oil and gas;\nstrong human capital; engineered resilience\n\nPoints worth knowing (p. 172–175):\n• SSP2 is not a forecast. The authors say it ”does not imply a simple extrapolation of recent experience” but a path consistent with historical patterns (p. 172).\n• SSP4 is the odd one out. It has no match in the older SRES scenarios (2014 paper, Table 2, p. 398) and the authors spend extra space justifying it using the inequality literature (p. 173–174).\n• SSP5 is the high-growth, high-fossil world. It assumes strong investment in health and education alongside heavy fossil use, and the authors note this combination has ”little historic precedent” (p. 174).\n• Each SSP is one story among many. The authors write: ”The storylines presented in this paper are canonical, but the canon is not exclusive” (section 5, last pages).\n• Open question. A world could move between domains, for example a technology surprise could shift SSP3 toward SSP4 (section 5)."},{"pdf":45,"printed":30,"ch":2,"text":"How RCPs and SSPs combine\nThe labels you see in the IPCC reports join the two: SSP1-2.6 means the SSP1 society with a radiative forcing of 2.6 W/m² by 2100. Not every pairing is plausible. A high-forcing world is hard to reach from SSP1, and a very low-forcing world is hard to reach from SSP3 or SSP5. The 2014 paper treats the combination as something to be built with the policy axis (p. 388), not as automatic.\n\nQuotables\nTable 2.7. The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\nSSPs are ”‘reference’ pathways in that they\nassume no climate change or climate impacts,\nand no new climate policies”\n\nPaper,\npage\n2014, p.\n389\n\n”they may not describe plausible assumptions for 2014, p.\nthe future, but this is an intentional component 389\nof the design”\n\nWhere it could be used\nDefining the SSPs; explaining why\ntransition risk needs a policy layer\nadded\nDefending the use of an SSP baseline\nwithout calling it a forecast\n\n”the SSPs should be seen as hypothetical\ndevelopment pathways that serve as a starting\npoint for developing integrated scenarios of the\nfuture, rather than as plausible scenarios\nthemselves”\n\n2014, p.\n399\n\nSame, in the authors' conclusion\n\nSocio-economic challenges to adaptation are\n”societal or environmental conditions that, by\nmaking adaptation more difficult, increase the\nrisks associated with any given projection of\nclimate change”\n\n2014, p.\n392\n\nBridging to the\nhazard–exposure–vulnerability risk\nframework\n\nSSP2 ”does not imply a simple extrapolation of\nrecent experience”\n\n2017, p.\n172\n\nIf you use SSP2 as a middle case\n\n”The storylines presented in this paper are\ncanonical, but the canon is not exclusive.”\n\n2017,\nsection 5\n\nNoting that SSP results depend on one\nparticular story\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• Where transition risk lives. The SSPs deliberately leave climate policy out; policy is a third, separate axis (the SPAs). Transition risk for banks is largely a policy-andtechnology question, so an SSP gives you the economic backdrop but not the transition shock itself.\n• India in the framework. SSP choice matters for exposure: population, income growth, urbanisation and energy demand differ across SSPs, and these set the size of India's future energy and credit demand. (Which SSP best fits India is a judgement you will need to argue; the papers here do not choose one for India.)\n• Mitigation versus adaptation challenge. The pair maps onto your two risk families: high mitigation challenge, such as SSP3 and SSP5, points to heavier transition risk if policy tightens late; high adaptation challenge, such as SSP3 and SSP4, points to heavier physical risk and weaker repayment capacity."},{"pdf":46,"printed":31,"ch":2,"text":"Can you skip the originals? For the concepts, yes. Open paper (a) if you want the argument for the inverse approach (section 3, p. 390–394) or Table 1 on drivers, and paper (b) if you want the full narratives (the supporting information has longer versions). Check any quote against the original before it goes into the thesis.\n\n3. IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023)\nSource: IPCC (2023), Climate Change 2023: Synthesis Report, edited by H. Lee and J. Romero. Your folder has two files: ”IPCC_AR6_SYR_FullVolume.pdf” (186 pages) and ”IPCC_AR6_SYR_LongerReport.pdf” (81 pages, which is printed pages 35–115 of the full volume). Page numbers below use the report's own numbering. ”SPM p. x” means the Summary for Policymakers (printed pages 1–34 of the full volume); ”Longer Report p. x” means printed pages 35–115. What I read. I read the whole Summary for Policymakers text, apart from the figures' fine detail. From the Longer Report I pulled only the passages on stranded assets and finance, found by searching, not by reading the whole section. Where a claim is from the Longer Report, I say so. The Synthesis Report is the IPCC's one-document answer to three questions: what has happened, what happens next under different emission paths, and what must change this decade. It combines the three Working Group reports of the Sixth Assessment Report, 2021–2022 (WG1 physical science, WG2 impacts and adaptation, WG3 mitigation). Its message for your thesis is that the 1.5°C and 2°C paths need deep emission cuts by 2030, and that fossil assets already built or planned do not fit inside them.\n\nVocabulary you need before reading it\nTable 2.8. IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023): Vocabulary you need before reading it Term\n\nMeaning\n\nCalibrated language\n\nWords like ”likely” or ”very likely” and confidence levels (”high\nconfidence”) are fixed IPCC scales. Ranges in the SPM are very likely\nranges (5–95%) unless stated (SPM p. 4, note 5).\n\nGlobal warming level\n(GWL)\n\nA temperature rise above 1850–1900, such as 1.5°C or 2°C. The IPCC\ncompares impacts at a GWL regardless of the year it is reached (Box\nSPM.1, p. 9).\n\nCO2-eq, GWP100\n\nPutting different greenhouse gases in one unit, using each gas's warming\nover 100 years (SPM p. 4, note 9).\n\nCarbon budget\n\nThe total CO2 that can still be emitted for a given chance of staying\nbelow a temperature. About 0.45°C of warming per 1000 GtCO2 emitted\n(B.5.2, p. 19).\n\nNet zero CO2\n\nCO2 added to the air equals CO2 removed. Reached earlier than net zero\nfor all greenhouse gases, because some hard-to-abate emissions remain\n(B.6.2, p. 21).\n\nCarbon dioxide removal\n(CDR)\n\nMethods that pull CO2 out of the air, such as reforestation or BECCS.\nNeeded to offset residual emissions."},{"pdf":47,"printed":32,"ch":2,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning\n\nOvershoot\n\nWarming goes above a target level and is later brought back down (B.7,\np. 23).\n\nSSPx-y\n\nA scenario from society SSPx with y W/m² of forcing in 2100. For\nexample SSP1-2.6 (footnote 21, p. 9). The SSP forcing tends to be\nhigher than the RCP with the same label (footnote 23, p. 9).\n\nWG3 categories C1–C8\n\nWG3 sorts 1,202 modelled pathways by warming outcome: C1 limits\nwarming to 1.5°C (over 50%) with no or limited overshoot, through to C8\nexceeding 4°C (Box SPM.1, Table 1, p. 10).\n\nNDC\n\nNationally Determined Contribution: a country's own Paris Agreement\npledge.\n\nEmissions gap versus\nimplementation gap\n\nGap between NDCs and the cuts needed for 1.5°C or 2°C, versus the gap\nbetween policies actually in place and NDCs (A.4.3 and A.4.4, p. 11).\n\nUnabated fossil fuels\n\nFossil fuels used without capturing most of the CO2, for example 90% or\nmore at a power plant (Longer Report p. 95, note).\n\nLock-in, stranded assets\n\nInfrastructure built now that keeps emitting for decades; assets that lose\nvalue before the end of their expected life because they cannot be used\nunder climate limits.\n\nReasons for Concern\n\nFive categories used to rate global climate risk: unique and threatened\nsystems, extreme weather, distribution of impacts, global aggregate\nimpacts, large-scale singular events (p. 15, note 38).\n\nThe argument in seven steps\n1. The warming is human-caused and already large. Global surface temperature was 1.09°C above 1850–1900 in 2011–2020, 1.59°C over land, and rising faster since 1970 than in any 50-year period in at least 2,000 years (A.1.1, p. 4). Net greenhouse gas emissions were 59 ± 6.6 GtCO2-eq in 2019, 54% above 1990 (A.1.4, p. 4).\n2. Impacts are already widespread and unequal. About 3.3 to 3.6 billion people live in contexts highly vulnerable to climate change (A.2.2, p. 5). Economic damages have been detected in agriculture, forestry, fishery, energy and tourism (A.2.6, p. 6).\n3. The scenarios. Box SPM.1 (p. 9) explains the SSP-based scenarios. Best estimates of warming for 2081–2100 are 1.4°C (SSP1-1.9), 1.8°C (SSP1-2.6), 2.7°C (SSP2-4.5), 3.6°C (SSP3-7.0) and 4.4°C (SSP5-8.5) (B.1.1 and note 30, p. 12). Warming is more likely than not to reach 1.5°C in the near term (2021–2040) even under SSP1-1.9.\n4. Risk grows with every increment of warming, and is higher than the previous report assessed. ”Many climate-related risks are higher than assessed in AR5” (B.2, p. 14). Risks also compound and cascade across sectors and regions (B.2.3, p. 15). For any given warming, risk depends on exposure and vulnerability, which are rising with urbanisation, migration and inequality (B.2.4, p. 15).\n5. The carbon budget is almost spent. From the start of 2020 the budget is about 500 GtCO2 for a 50% chance of 1.5°C and 1,150 GtCO2 for a 67% chance of 2°C (B.5.2, p. 19). CO2 from existing fossil infrastructure, without extra abatement, already exceeds the 1.5°C budget (B.5.3, p. 20).\n6. The paths that work require cuts now. Pathways limiting warming to 1.5°C or 2°C involve rapid, deep and mostly immediate cuts this decade, reaching net zero CO2 in the"},{"pdf":48,"printed":33,"ch":2,"text":"early 2050s and the early 2070s respectively (B.6, p. 20). Table SPM.1 (p. 21) gives the cut from 2019 levels: Table 2.9. IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023): The argument in seven steps Cut from 2019 level\n\n2030\n\n2035\n\n2040\n\n2050\n\n1.5°C (over 50%), all greenhouse gases\n\n43%\n\n60%\n\n69%\n\n84%\n\n1.5°C (over 50%), CO2 only\n\n48%\n\n65%\n\n80%\n\n99%\n\n2°C (over 67%), all greenhouse gases\n\n21%\n\n35%\n\n46%\n\n64%\n\n2°C (over 67%), CO2 only\n\n22%\n\n37%\n\n51%\n\n73%\n\n7. Current pledges and policies fall short. Pledges made before COP26 (the November 2021 UN climate conference) would lead to median warming of 2.8°C by 2100 (A.4.3, p. 11); policies actually implemented by end-2020 lead to 3.2°C (A.4.4, p. 11). Delay would ”lock in high-emissions infrastructure, raise risks of stranded assets and cost-escalation” (C.2, p. 25).\n\nThe parts that matter most for a banking thesis\n• Stranded assets. In the SPM: delayed action raises ”risks of stranded assets” (C.2, p. 25; C.2.2, p. 26). In the Longer Report: limiting warming to 2°C or below ”could strand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure”, with a globally discounted value of around USD 1 to 4 trillion from 2015 to 2050 (medium confidence). Early action keeps the stranded amount smaller; delayed action with continued investment in unabated high-emitting infrastructure before 2030 pushes it to the high end (Longer Report p. 95). The Longer Report also says about 80% of coal, 50% of gas and 30% of oil reserves cannot be burned if warming is limited to 2°C (Longer Report p. 58).\n• Cheaper alternatives. From 2010 to 2019 unit costs fell 85% for solar, 55% for wind and 85% for lithium-ion batteries (A.4.2, p. 11). The report notes that keeping emissionintensive systems may be more expensive than switching in some regions and sectors.\n• Finance flows are the wrong way round. ”Public and private finance flows for fossil fuels are still greater than those for climate adaptation and mitigation” (A.4.5, p. 11). Mitigation investment in 1.5°C and 2°C scenarios would need to be three to six times current levels for 2020–2030 (C.7.2, p. 33).\n• The financial sector is named. There is enough global capital, but barriers to redirecting it. ”Investors, financial intermediaries, central banks and financial regulators can shift the systemic underpricing of climate-related risks” (C.7.3, p. 33; same wording in the Longer Report p. 112).\n• Carbon pricing alone is weak. Where used, carbon pricing instruments ”have incentivized low-cost emissions reduction measures but have been less effective, on their own and at prevailing prices” for higher-cost measures (C.6.4, p. 32).\n• Benefits versus costs. The global economic and social benefit of limiting warming to 2°C exceeds the cost of mitigation in most of the assessed literature (medium confidence), though cost-benefit analysis is limited in what it can represent (C.2.4, p. 26).\n\nQuotables"},{"pdf":49,"printed":34,"ch":2,"text":"Table 2.10. IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\n”These are quantitative projections and are\nneither predictions nor forecasts.”\n\nSPM p. 9 (Box\nSPM.1)\n\nAny time you describe scenarios\nas inputs, not forecasts\n\n”Delayed mitigation and adaptation action\nwould lock in high-emissions infrastructure,\nraise risks of stranded assets and\ncost-escalation”\n\nSPM p. 25 (C.2)\n\nIntroducing stranded-asset risk\nwith an authoritative source\n\n”Projected CO2 emissions from existing fossil\nfuel infrastructure without additional\nabatement would exceed the remaining\ncarbon budget for 1.5°C (50%)”\n\nSPM p. 19 (B.5\nsummary); also\nB.5.3, p. 20\n\nThe core physical argument\nbehind transition risk\n\n”Public and private finance flows for fossil\nfuels are still greater than those for climate\nadaptation and mitigation”\n\nSPM p. 11 (A.4.5) Showing finance has not yet\nrealigned\n\n”Investors, financial intermediaries, central\nSPM p. 33 (C.7.3) Justifying a role for regulators\nbanks and financial regulators can shift the\nand capital rules\nsystemic underpricing of climate-related risks”\nStranded fossil assets ”around USD 1 to 4\ntrillion from 2015 to 2050”\n\nLonger Report p.\n\nA global magnitude for stranded\nassets (note: medium confidence)\n\n”Global net anthropogenic GHG emissions\nhave been estimated to be 59 ± 6.6\nGtCO2-eq in 2019”\n\nSPM p. 4 (A.1.4)\n\nA baseline emissions figure\n\nMitigation investment requirements ”a factor\nof three to six greater than current levels”\n\nSPM p. 33 (C.7.2) The scale of the financing gap\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• A source for the stranded-asset logic. The cleanest chain is: the carbon budget is small (B.5.2), existing infrastructure already exceeds it (B.5.3), so some assets cannot be used for their full life (Longer Report p. 95). That is the physical reason a transition shock to banks' loan books is plausible.\n• Timing matters more than level. The report stresses that delay raises the strandedasset total and the cost. For a bank, a late and sharp policy response is worse than an early and gradual one. (That bank-level implication is my inference, not an IPCC statement.)\n• What the IPCC does not give you. The Synthesis Report has no sector-by-sector loan analysis, no bank risk model, and nothing India-specific on capital adequacy. It is the scientific and scenario backdrop; the banking mechanics come from Layers 3 to 6.\n• India context. Asia appears among regions projected to face increasing losses and damages (C.2.2, p. 26). For the global net-zero timing that frames India's 2070 pledge, see B.6, p. 20 (early 2070s for 2°C). Can you skip the original? For the argument and numbers, yes. Still open these four places yourself: Box SPM.1 with Table 1 (p. 9–10), Figure SPM.4 ”burning embers” (p. 17– 18), Table SPM.1 (p. 21), and Longer Report p. 95 for the stranded-asset passage, because you will probably quote them. I did not read the rest of the Longer Report, which gives the"},{"pdf":50,"printed":35,"ch":2,"text":"evidence behind each SPM statement. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n4. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: Summary\nfor Policymakers (2022)\nSource: IPCC (2022), Climate Change 2022: Mitigation of Climate Change, Working Group III contribution to the Sixth Assessment Report, Summary for Policymakers (your file ”IPCC_AR6_WGIII_SummaryForPolicymakers.pdf”, 56 PDF pages; the summary itself is printed pages 3 to about 50). Page numbers below are the summary's own, which run four lower than the PDF page. What I read. I read sections B (trends, B.1 to B.7), C.1 to C.4 (pathways, net zero, energy) and E.4 to E.5 (policy and finance). I did not read section D, the other parts of C and E, or the figure panels in detail. This is the report that answers ”how much must emissions fall, by when, and what happens to fossil assets?”. It is also the source behind the Synthesis Report's mitigation numbers, so for your thesis it is the primary citation for stranded assets and for the gap between pledges and the Paris goals.\n\nTerms specific to this report\nTable 2.11. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: Terms specific to this report Term\n\nMeaning\n\nImmediate action\n\nClimate policies adopted between 2020 and at the latest before 2025 to\nlimit warming to a given level (note 26, p. 14). All assessed 1.5°C\npathways assume it.\n\nC1, C2, C3a\n\nPathway categories. C1: 1.5°C (over 50%) with no or limited overshoot.\nC2: back to 1.5°C after high overshoot. C3a: 2°C (over 67%) with\nimmediate action (Table SPM.2).\n\nUnconditional and\nconditional NDC elements\n\nUnconditional: pledges made without conditions. Conditional: pledges\nthat depend on finance, technology transfer or cooperation (note 27, p.\n14). India's 2031–2035 NDC makes part of its commitments conditional\non such support (from the NDC document, covered in the earlier\nreading plan).\n\nImplementation gap\n\nGap between emissions from policies actually in place and those\nimplied by NDCs (B.6.1, p. 14).\n\nEmissions gap\n\nGap between NDC-implied emissions and what 1.5°C or 2°C pathways\nneed (B.6.1, p. 14).\n\nUnabated fossil fuels\n\nFossil fuel use without capturing most of the CO2 (note 54).\n\nCCS\n\nCarbon capture and storage: capture CO2 at a plant or industrial site\nand store it underground.\n\nIllustrative Mitigation\nPathways (IMPs)\n\nFive worked examples of routes to the same temperature, for example\nheavy reliance on net-negative emissions versus high resource efficiency\n(C.3, p. 24)."},{"pdf":51,"printed":36,"ch":2,"text":"What the report finds\n1. Emissions are still rising, with a slowdown (B.1, p. 6). Net global greenhouse gas emissions were 59 ± 6.6 GtCO2-eq in 2019, 12% above 2010 and 54% above 1990. The 2010–2019 average was the highest decadal level on record, though growth slowed from 2.1% a year to 1.3% a year. Energy supply produced about 34% of 2019 emissions and industry 24% (B.2.1, p. 8).\n2. The carbon budget is small compared with past emissions (B.1.3, p. 6–7).\nCumulative net CO2 from 1850 to 2019 was 2,400 ± 240 GtCO2. The remaining budget from 2020 is 500 GtCO2 for a 50% chance of 1.5°C and 1,150 GtCO2 for a 67% chance of 2°C. Emissions in 2010–2019 alone equal about four-fifths of the 1.5°C remaining budget.\n3. Clean technology costs have fallen (B.4.1, p. 11). From 2010 to 2019 unit costs fell 85% for solar, 55% for wind and 85% for lithium-ion batteries. The report notes that large-scale technologies with fewer chances to learn ”have seen minimal cost reductions”.\n4. Pledges are not enough (B.6, p. 14). NDCs announced before COP26 make it\nlikely that warming will exceed 1.5°C, and holding it below 2°C ”would then rely on a rapid acceleration of mitigation efforts after 2030”. Policies implemented by end-2020 give higher emissions than NDCs. Table SPM.1 (p. 14), in GtCO2-eq per year in 2030: Table 2.12. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds Measure\n\nValue\n\nMedian emissions under policies implemented by end-2020\n\n57 (range 52–60)\n\nMedian emissions under NDCs, unconditional elements only\n\n53 (range 50–57)\n\nMedian emissions under NDCs including conditional elements\n\n50 (range 47–55)\n\nEmissions gap to 2°C pathways (immediate action)\n\n10–16 unconditional; 6–14 with\nconditional\n\nEmissions gap to 1.5°C pathways (immediate action)\n\n19–26 unconditional; 16–23 with\nconditional\n\n5. Existing and planned fossil infrastructure already uses up the budget (B.7, p. 16). Projected cumulative future CO2 from existing fossil infrastructure, if operated as it has been and without extra abatement, is 660 [460–890] GtCO2 from 2018 to the end of its life. Adding planned power-sector infrastructure gives 850 [600–1,100] GtCO2. The comparison: Table 2.13. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds (b) Quantity\n\nGtCO2\n\nFuture CO2 from existing fossil infrastructure\n\n660\n\nSame, plus planned power-sector infrastructure\n\n850\n\nCumulative net CO2 to net zero in 1.5°C pathways\n\n510\n\nCumulative net CO2 to net zero in 2°C pathways\n\n890\n\nSo existing infrastructure alone exceeds the 1.5°C path (B.7), and existing plus planned infrastructure is roughly equal to the 2°C path. This is the quantitative core of the stranded-asset argument."},{"pdf":52,"printed":37,"ch":2,"text":"6. Emissions must peak and then fall fast (C.1, p. 17). Emissions peak between 2020 and at the latest before 2025 in 1.5°C pathways, and in 2°C pathways with immediate action. Without stronger policy, the median warming is 3.2 [2.2 to 3.5]°C by 2100. Required cuts from 2019 (C.1.1, p. 17): Table 2.14. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds (c) Pathway\n\nBy 2030\n\nBy 2050\n\n1.5°C (C1)\n\n43% [34–60]\n\n84% [73–98]\n\n2°C with immediate action (C3a)\n\n27% [13–45]\n\n63% [52–76]\n\nReturn to 1.5°C after high overshoot (C2)\n\n23% [0–44]\n\n75% [62–91]\n\nNote on numbers: the Synthesis Report's Table SPM.1 gives 21% for 2030 for 2°C because it covers the wider C3 category; the 27% above is for C3a (immediate action). Quote the one that matches the category you mean.\n7. Net zero dates (C.2, p. 23). Net zero CO2 comes in the early 2050s in 1.5°C pathways and around the early 2070s in 2°C pathways, with cumulative net CO2 until net zero of 510 GtCO2 and 890 GtCO2 respectively (C.2.1).\n8. Fossil fuel use must fall steeply by 2050 (C.3.2, p. 24). Median decline in global use relative to 2019: Table 2.15. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds (d) Fuel\n\n1.5°C pathways\n\n2°C pathways\n\nCoal\n\nabout 95%\n\n85%\n\nOil\n\nabout 60%\n\n30%\n\nGas\n\nabout 45%\n\n15%\n\nIn 2050 almost all electricity comes from zero- or low-carbon sources.\n9. Stranded assets, with timing (C.4.4, p. 28–29). ”Limiting global warming to 2°C or below will leave a substantial amount of fossil fuels unburned and could strand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure.” The combined discounted value of unburned fossil fuels and stranded infrastructure is projected at around USD 1–4 trillion from 2015 to 2050 for about 2°C, and higher for 1.5°C (medium confidence). Coal assets are at risk of being stranded before 2030; oil and gas assets nearer mid-century. CCS, if available, could allow fossil fuels to be used longer.\n10. Policy and finance (E.4 to E.5, p. 46–47). Carbon pricing alone, at prevailing prices, was not enough for higher-cost measures. Investment needs for 2020–2030 in 2°C or 1.5°C scenarios are three to six times current levels. There is enough global capital, but barriers stop it moving, including ”inadequate assessment of climate-related risks and investment opportunities” (E.5.2).\n\nQuotables"},{"pdf":53,"printed":38,"ch":2,"text":"Table 2.16. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\n”Limiting global warming to 2°C or below will\nC.4.4, p.\nleave a substantial amount of fossil fuels unburned 28–29\nand could strand considerable fossil fuel\ninfrastructure”\n\nDefining stranded-asset risk\n\n”coal assets are projected to be at risk of being\nstranded before 2030, while oil and gas assets are\nprojected to be more at risk of being stranded\ntowards mid-century”\n\nC.4.4, p.\n28–29\n\nTiming of transition risk; relevant for\ncoal-heavy exposures\n\nExisting and planned fossil infrastructure ”exceed\nthe total cumulative net CO2 emissions in\npathways that limit warming to 1.5°C”\n\nB.7, p.\n\nThe carbon-budget argument in one\nsentence\n\n”Likely limiting warming to below 2°C would then B.6, p.\nrely on a rapid acceleration of mitigation efforts\nafter 2030.”\n\nWhy a late, sharp policy shift is a\nplausible scenario\n\n”Maintaining emission-intensive systems may, in\nsome regions and sectors, be more expensive than\ntransitioning to low emission systems.”\n\nC.4.2, p.\n\nCounter-argument to the idea that\ntransition is always costly\n\n”inadequate assessment of climate-related risks\nand investment opportunities”\n\nE.5.2, p.\n\nMotivating better risk assessment\ninside financial institutions\n\nNet zero CO2 ”in the early 2050s” (1.5°C) and\n”around the early 2070s” (2°C)\n\nC.2, p.\n\nGlobal timing context for India's 2070\npledge\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• The strongest single source for the stranded-asset case. Use B.7 for the quantity (660 to 850 GtCO2 against budgets of 510 and 890) and C.4.4 for the value (USD 1–4 trillion) and the timing (coal first).\n• Scenario design. The 2030 emission levels and the cut percentages in C.1.1 give you numbers to anchor an orderly versus delayed transition story. (The orderly/disorderly labels are a central-bank scenario convention, not IPCC wording.)\n• Policy uncertainty is part of the picture. The gap between implemented policies (57 GtCO2-eq) and NDCs (50 to 53) means policy outcomes are themselves uncertain, which feeds directly into how much transition risk a bank faces.\n• India. The report is global and does not give a bank-by-bank or India-specific strandedasset estimate. India's 2070 net zero sits at the 2°C-pathway date (early 2070s) in C.2. India's own conditionality of NDC elements comes from its NDC document, not from this report. Can you skip the original? For the numbers above, yes. Open the document itself for Table SPM.2 (the full list of pathway categories with temperatures and net zero dates), B.7 (p. 16) and C.4.4 (p. 28–29), because you will probably quote them. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis."},{"pdf":54,"printed":39,"ch":2,"text":"5. IPCC Working Group 2, Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability\n(2022)\nSource: IPCC (2022), Climate Change 2022: Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability, Working Group II contribution to the Sixth Assessment Report (your file ”IPCC_AR6_WGII_FullReport.pdf”, 3,068 PDF pages, about 380 MB). Page references: ”SPM p. x” is the report's own Summary for Policymakers numbering (PDF page minus 12); ”Ch. 10 p. x” and ”Ch. 16 p. x” use the report's running page numbers. What I read. I read the Summary for Policymakers sections A and B (introduction, observed impacts, vulnerability, near-term and long-term risks, compound risks, overshoot) in full. I read the executive summaries of Chapter 10 (Asia) and Chapter 16 (Key Risks across Sectors and Regions). I searched the rest of the report for banking and finance terms. I did not read the other 3,000 pages, nor section C onward of the summary (adaptation and resilient development). This is the report that supplies the risk framework your thesis will lean on: risk is hazard, exposure and vulnerability together, and each can change independently. That is why the same physical hazard can mean very different losses for different borrowers.\n\nThe framework in the IPCC's own terms\nTable 2.17. IPCC Working Group 2, Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability (2022): The framework in the IPCC's own terms Term\n\nDefinition (paraphrased from the SPM notes)\n\nRisk\n\nThe potential for adverse consequences for human or ecological systems (note\n5, p. 5).\n\nHazard\n\nA natural or human-induced physical event or trend that may cause loss,\ninjury or damage. WG1 measures the physical side as ”climatic\nimpact-drivers” (note 6, p. 5).\n\nExposure\n\nThe presence of people, assets, infrastructure, ecosystems or livelihoods in\nplaces that could be adversely affected (note 7, p. 5).\n\nVulnerability\n\nThe propensity or predisposition to be adversely affected; includes sensitivity and lack of capacity to cope and adapt (note 8, p. 5).\n\nKey risk\n\nA risk with potentially severe consequences from hazards interacting with the\nvulnerability of exposed systems (note 9, p. 5). The report identifies 127 of\nthem.\n\nAdaptation limits\n\nHard limit: no adaptive action can avoid intolerable risk. Soft limit: options exist but are not currently available (note 11, p. 7).\n\nMaladaptation\n\nActions that increase risk of adverse climate outcomes, including through\nmore emissions or shifted vulnerability (note 15, p. 7).\n\nReasons for Concern\n\nFive categories of global risk, used in the ”burning embers” diagram (p. 13\nand Figure SPM.3b).\n\nOvershoot\n\nWarming exceeds 1.5°C and returns to or below it later (note 27, p. 8).\n\nLosses and damages\n\nObserved impacts or projected risks, economic and non-economic (note 19, p.\n7)."},{"pdf":55,"printed":40,"ch":2,"text":"Figure SPM.1 (p. 6) draws this as a ”risk propeller”: risk emerges from the overlap of climate hazard, exposure and vulnerability. Human responses can themselves add risk. The Introduction (p. 5) says so directly: ”The risk that can be introduced by human responses to climate change is a new aspect considered in the risk concept.”\n\nWhat the summary finds\n1. Impacts are already widespread, and unequal (B.1 and B.2, p. 9 and 12).\nAdverse impacts attributed to human-induced climate change are widespread and pervasive. About 3.3 to 3.6 billion people live in contexts highly vulnerable to climate change. Human vulnerability hotspots include South Asia. Between 2010 and 2020, mortality from floods, droughts and storms was 15 times higher in highly vulnerable regions than in regions with very low vulnerability (B.2.4, p. 12).\n2. Vulnerability and exposure are partly driven by development choices (B.2, p. 12). Unsustainable development patterns raise exposure. Future exposure increases with migration, inequality and urbanisation (B.2.5, p. 12–13). Key infrastructure (water, transport, energy, communications) becomes more vulnerable if design standards ignore a changing climate.\n3. In the near term, who and what is exposed matters more than the emissions\nscenario (B.3.2, p. 13). Risk to systems ”depend more strongly on changes in their vulnerability and exposure than on differences in climate hazards between emissions scenarios.” Many near-term risks are unavoidable whichever scenario happens.\n4. Risk thresholds are lower than previously thought (B.3.3, p. 13). Very high risk emerges in all five Reasons for Concern between 1.2°C and 4.5°C of warming, against two of five in the previous assessment. Risk to unique and threatened systems moves from high to very high at a median of 1.5°C, and risk from extreme weather at a median of 2.0°C.\n5. Mid- to long-term impacts grow with every increment (B.4, p. 14). For the 127 key risks, assessed impacts are up to multiple times higher than currently observed. Flood damages without adaptation are 1.4 to 2 times higher at 2°C and 2.5 to 3.9 times higher at 3°C than at 1.5°C (B.4.2, p. 14).\n6. Assets at risk (B.4.5, p. 15). By 2100 the value of global assets within the future 1-in100-year coastal floodplains is projected to be US$7.9 to US$12.7 trillion (2011 value) under RCP4.5, and US$8.8 to US$14.2 trillion under RCP8.5 (medium confidence).\n7. Economic damages are uncertain and rise non-linearly (B.4.6, p. 15). The report says the range of global damage estimates, and the lack of comparability between methods, ”does not allow for identification of a robust range of estimates”. Damages per person are often larger as a share of income in developing countries.\n8. Risks compound and cascade (B.5, p. 18–19). Simultaneous hazards and interacting risks can compound overall risk. Impacts also cross borders through supply chains, markets and natural resource flows (B.5.3, p. 19).\n9. Overshoot is costly (B.6, p. 19). If warming transiently exceeds 1.5°C, many systems face additional severe risks and some impacts are irreversible even after warming falls again."},{"pdf":56,"printed":41,"ch":2,"text":"Asia and India (Chapter 10 executive summary, p. 1459–1461)\n• Energy and industry. ”Among 13 developing countries with large energy consumption in Asia, 11 are exposed to high-energy insecurity and industrial-systems risk” (p. 1459).\n• Water. By mid-century the transboundary basins of the Amu Darya, Indus and Ganges could face severe water scarcity (p. 1459).\n• Agriculture. In India, rice production can decrease by 10 to 30% and maize by 25 to 70% for a temperature rise of 1°C to 4°C (p. 1460).\n• Cities. Asia has the largest share of people living in informal settlements (197 million in Central and Southern Asia). Coastal cities in South and Southeast Asia face large increases in average annual economic losses from flooding between 2005 and 2050. By 2080, 940 million to 1.1 billion urban dwellers in South and Southeast Asia could be affected by extreme heat lasting more than 30 days a year (p. 1460).\n• Barriers to resilience. The chapter lists fragmented, reactive governance, inadequate evidence on what to prioritise, and finance deficits (p. 1461).\n\nEconomics (Chapter 16 executive summary, p. 2414)\nExtreme weather events ”not only cause substantial direct economic damage” but also reduce economic growth in the short term and, with medium confidence, in the long term, up to 15 years after the event, with more severe impacts in developing than in industrialised economies.\n\nQuotables\nTable 2.18. IPCC Working Group 2, Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability (2022): Quotables Quote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\nRisk ”can arise from the dynamic interactions\namong climate-related hazards”, ”the exposure\nand vulnerability of affected human and\necological systems” (footnote markers omitted)\n\nSPM p. 5 Defining physical risk with the IPCC\nframework\n\n”The risk that can be introduced by human\nresponses to climate change is a new aspect\nconsidered in the risk concept.”\n\nSPM p. 5 Justifying treating transition (response)\nrisk as part of climate risk\n\nNear-term risks ”depend more strongly on\nSPM p.\nArguing that exposure data matter\nchanges in their vulnerability and exposure than 13 (B.3.2) more than scenario choice for near-term on differences in climate hazards between bank risk emissions scenarios” ”Multiple climate hazards will occur simultaneously, and multiple climatic and non-climatic risks will interact, resulting in compounding overall risk and risks cascading across sectors and regions.”\n\nSPM p.\n18 (B.5)\n\nMotivating interaction and contagion\neffects beyond a single-factor model\n\n”Weather and climate extremes are causing\nSPM p.\nCross-border and supply-chain channels\neconomic and societal impacts across national\n19 (B.5.3)\nboundaries through supply-chains, markets, and\nnatural resource flows”"},{"pdf":57,"printed":42,"ch":2,"text":"Quote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\nThe range of global damage estimates and ”lack SPM p.\nAcknowledging uncertainty in damage\nof comparability between methodologies” ”does 15 (B.4.6) functions\nnot allow for identification of a robust range of\nestimates”\n”In India, rice production can decrease from 10\nto 30%, whereas maize production can decrease\nfrom 25 to 70%” for 1°C to 4°C\n\nCh. 10, p. Agricultural credit exposure in India\n1460\n\nExtreme weather events reduce economic\ngrowth ”in the short term” and ”in the long\nterm (up to 15 years after the event)”\n\nCh. 16, p. Linking physical shocks to borrowers'\n2414\nrepayment capacity\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• A ready physical-risk framework. Hazard, exposure and vulnerability give you a clean way to separate what the climate does from what a bank's borrowers are exposed to. The IPCC treats exposure and vulnerability as changing independently over time, so a loan book's physical risk can change without any change in the climate.\n• Transition risk has IPCC cover. The ”risk from human responses” wording (p. 5) lets you present transition risk as part of the same risk concept, rather than as a separate idea (my reading of how the passage can be used).\n• Where this report is thin. WG2 has no bank or capital-adequacy analysis, and the search of the full report found few passages on the financial sector. The finance mechanics come from Layers 3 to 6. India appears through the Asia chapter, mainly for agriculture, water, energy and cities.\n• Caution on numbers. Many WG2 results are ”without additional adaptation” or\nmedium confidence. Quote the confidence level with the figure.\nCan you skip the original? For concepts and headline findings, yes. Open these yourself: Figure SPM.1 (p. 6, the risk propeller), Figure SPM.3 (p. 16–17, global and regional risks and the ”burning embers”), and B.3.2 (p. 13) if you plan to quote it. I did not read Chapter 10 or 16 beyond their executive summaries, so for anything India-specific go to the body of Chapter\n10. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n6. Master quotables list and how Layer 1 feeds the thesis\nThe ten quotes below are the ones I would reach for first. Each is repeated from its section above with the same page reference; the full tables in sections 1 to 5 hold more. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\nTop quotables, by job"},{"pdf":58,"printed":43,"ch":2,"text":"Table 2.19. Master quotables list and how Layer 1 feeds the thesis: Top quotables, by job Job in the thesis\n\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nSource and page\n\nScenarios are not forecasts ”These are quantitative projections and are neither predictions nor forecasts.”\n\nAR6 SYR SPM p.\n9 (Box SPM.1)\n\nWhat an RCP is\n\n”The word ‘representative’ signifies that each of the\nRCPs represents a larger set of scenarios in the\nliterature.”\n\nRCP paper p. 7\n\nCaveat on RCP\ncomparisons\n\n”The RCPs should not be interpreted as forecasts or\nabsolute bounds, or be seen as policy prescriptive.”\n\nRCP paper p. 26\n\nWhy SSPs need a policy\nlayer\n\nSSPs are ”‘reference’ pathways in that they assume no SSP paper (2014)\nclimate change or climate impacts, and no new\np. 389\nclimate policies”\n\nDefending the SSP\nbaseline\n\n”they may not describe plausible assumptions for the\nfuture, but this is an intentional component of the\ndesign”\n\nSSP paper (2014)\np. 389\n\nStranded-asset risk,\ndefined\n\n”Limiting global warming to 2°C or below will leave a\nsubstantial amount of fossil fuels unburned and could\nstrand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure”\n\nWG3 SPM C.4.4,\np. 28–29\n\nTiming of stranding\n\nWG3 SPM C.4.4,\n”coal assets are projected to be at risk of being\np. 28–29\nstranded before 2030, while oil and gas assets are\nprojected to be more at risk of being stranded towards\nmid-century”\n\nThe carbon-budget\nargument\n\nExisting and planned fossil infrastructure ”exceed the\ntotal cumulative net CO2 emissions in pathways that\nlimit warming to 1.5°C”\n\nWG3 SPM B.7, p.\n\nRole of financial regulators ”Investors, financial intermediaries, central banks and financial regulators can shift the systemic underpricing of climate-related risks”\n\nAR6 SYR SPM p.\n33 (C.7.3)\n\nPhysical risk is exposure\nand vulnerability too\n\nNear-term risks ”depend more strongly on changes in WG2 SPM p. 13\ntheir vulnerability and exposure than on differences in (B.3.2)\nclimate hazards between emissions scenarios”\n\nHow the five sources fit together\nTable 2.20. Master quotables list and how Layer 1 feeds the thesis: How the five sources fit together Thesis question\n\nWhere to look\n\nWhat you get\n\nHow much warming for a given\nemissions path?\n\nSection 1 (RCP\npaper)\n\nThe physical side only: forcing levels 2.6 to 8.5\nW/m².\n\nWhat kind of world are we in?\n\nSection 2 (SSP\npapers)\n\nFive development pathways, with no climate\npolicy built in.\n\nHow do scenario labels and\nglobal numbers line up?\n\nSection 3\n(Synthesis\nReport)\n\nWarming levels, carbon budgets, net zero dates,\nthe 1–4 trillion stranded-asset range.\n\nHow big is the transition and\nwhich assets are hit?\n\nSection 4 (WG3\nSPM)\n\nRequired cuts, fossil fuel decline, stranded-asset\ntiming, policy and finance."},{"pdf":59,"printed":44,"ch":2,"text":"Thesis question\n\nWhere to look\n\nWhat you get\n\nWhat does physical risk look\nlike, and for India?\n\nSection 5 (WG2) Hazard, exposure and vulnerability; Asia and\nIndia findings; compounding risk.\n\nFour ideas to carry out of Layer 1\n1. Physical and transition risk need different sources. The RCPs and WG2 describe what the climate does and what it hits. The transition shock comes from policy and technology, which the SSPs deliberately leave out (SSP paper p. 389) and which WG3 describes.\n2. Scenarios are inputs, not predictions. Say so with the Box SPM.1 quote, and say which scenario you use and why.\n3. Timing matters as much as the level. Late and sharp action raises stranded-asset losses and costs (SYR C.2; WG3 B.6). The orderly versus disorderly labels are a centralbank convention, not IPCC wording.\n4. None of these documents analyses banks. There is no capital-adequacy, loan-book or Indian public sector bank analysis in Layer 1. It gives the scientific and scenario backdrop; the banking mechanics come from Layers 3 to 6. India enters here only through the Asia chapter of WG2 and the 2070 net zero date (SYR B.6; WG3 C.2).\n\nWhat I did not read\nThe body of the Synthesis Report Longer Report beyond p. 95, the body of the WG3 report, the WG2 report beyond the Summary for Policymakers and the executive summaries of Chapters 10 and 16, and WG2 section C onward. If your thesis leans on one of those, tell me and I will read it next."},{"pdf":60,"printed":45,"ch":2,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 1"},{"pdf":61,"printed":46,"ch":2,"text":""},{"pdf":62,"printed":47,"ch":2,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 1"},{"pdf":63,"printed":48,"ch":2,"text":""}],"cards":[]};

window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.ln=window.CHTML.ln||{};window.CHTML.ln[2]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">How to use these notes, and a map of Layer 1</h1>\n<p data-b=\"2\">These notes are written so you can work from them and skip the originals for concepts and headline numbers. Each section follows the same pattern: a source line, vocabulary, the argument or findings, a quotables table (quote, page, where to use it), what it means for your thesis, and a verdict on whether you can skip the original. Where the verdict says to open a specific page, do that before quoting it.</p>\n<p data-b=\"3\"><strong>Two rules.</strong> First, check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis; I read from extracted text and the page numbers are the documents’ own printed numbers. Second, where I give my own inference rather than the source’ s claim, the text says so.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"4\" id=\"b4\">Before you start: what you need to know first</h2>\n<p data-b=\"5\">Layer 1 has almost no prerequisites. You need four climate ideas, and each is defined again in the section where it first matters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"6\"><strong>Emissions, concentration, forcing and temperature are four different things.</strong> Emissions are flows (GtCO2 a year); concentration is the stock in the air (ppm); radiative forcing is the energy imbalance that stock causes (W/m²); temperature is the response. The RCP names (2.6 to 8.5) are about forcing.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"7\"><strong>CO2-equivalent (CO2e)</strong> puts all greenhouse gases on one scale, so emission totals can be compared.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"8\"><strong>A cumulative budget</strong> exists because warming follows total CO2 emitted, not the yearly rate. This is why \"net zero\" dates matter.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"9\"><strong>A scenario is a conditional story, not a forecast.</strong> If you remember one thing, remember this.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"10\">Nothing in Layer 1 requires finance or economics. What Layer 1 does not give you is any link to banks; that comes from Layers 3 to 6.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"11\" id=\"b11\">The map</h2>\n<table data-b=\"12\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.1.</strong> How to use these notes, and a map of Layer 1: The map</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Section</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">One-line job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP paper (2011)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The physical scenarios: 2.6, 4.5, 6, 8.5 W/m².</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP papers (2014 and 2017)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Five development pathways with no climate policy; the policy axis comes separately.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IPCC AR6 Synthesis Report (2023)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The combined message: warming levels, carbon budgets, net zero timing, stranded assets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IPCC AR6 WG3 SPM (2022)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The transition side: cuts required, fossil fuel decline, stranded-asset timing, finance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IPCC AR6 WG2 (2022)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The physical risk side: hazard, exposure, vulnerability; Asia and India.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Master list</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The ten best quotes and how it all feeds the thesis.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"13\" id=\"b13\">Reading the scenario labels</h2>\n<p data-b=\"14\">A label like <strong>SSP1-2.6</strong> joins two ideas: the development pathway (SSP1) and the forcing level in W/m² reached by 2100 (2.6, from the RCP paper). IPCC also groups pathways by warming level and category (C1 to C8) in the WG3 and Synthesis Report tables. Sections 1 and 2 explain the pieces; section 3 shows how AR6 uses them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"15\" id=\"b15\">If you only have time for part of it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"16\">Read section 6 first (the ten quotes and the four ideas).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"17\">Then section 4, the strongest source for the stranded-asset case.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"18\">Then section 5 for the physical risk framework.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"19\">Sections 1 to 3 when you write the scenario chapter.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h1 data-b=\"20\" id=\"b20\">1. van Vuuren et al. (2011), The Representative Concentration Pathways</h1>\n<p data-b=\"21\"><strong>Source:</strong> van Vuuren, Edmonds, Kainuma, Riahi, Thomson, Hibbard, Hurtt, Kram, Krey, Lamarque, Masui, Meinshausen, Nakicenovic, Smith, Rose (2011), <em>Climatic Change</em> 109:5–31 (your file \"RCp.pdf\", 27 pages). Page numbers below are the journal’ s own.</p>\n<p data-b=\"22\">The paper explains where the four RCPs (RCP2.6, 4.5, 6 and 8.5) come from and what they are, and just as importantly what they are not. An RCP fixes how much extra heat the atmosphere traps by 2100 and does not describe a whole future world.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"23\" id=\"b23\">Why this paper exists</h2>\n<p data-b=\"24\">Climate models need a common set of future inputs, so that results from different labs can be compared. Older sets (IS92, SRES) did not give enough detail for modern climate models and did not include climate policy. The IPCC asked the research community to build a new set (p. 6–7). The community chose a three-phase plan (p. 7):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"25\">Build four pathways of emissions, concentrations and land use (the RCPs).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"26\">In parallel, run climate models on them and write new socio-economic scenarios (this became the SSPs, next section).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"27\">Integrate and disseminate.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"28\">The RCPs were deliberately built first so that climate modellers did not have to wait for the economists.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"29\" id=\"b29\">Technical terms in this paper</h2>\n<table data-b=\"30\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.2.</strong> van Vuuren et al. (2011): Technical terms in this paper</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Plain meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Radiative forcing (W/m²)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Extra energy the Earth traps per square metre, relative to pre-industrial times. Higher means more warming. This is the number in each RCP’ s name.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Concentration pathway</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A path of greenhouse-gas concentrations in the air over time, used as input to a climate model. The paper stresses it is an input, not a full scenario (p. 7).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IAM (integrated assessment model)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">An economy–energy–land–climate model used to ask how emissions would evolve under different technology and policy assumptions. Four IAM teams built the four RCPs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Harmonization</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Adjusting each model’ s output so that its starting year (2000 or 2005) matches observed historical data, then blending back to the model’ s own path (p. 14).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Downscaling</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Spreading regional model output onto a 0.5° × 0.5° grid, so climate models can use it (p. 13–14).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ECP (Extended Concentration Pathway)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A simple, stylised extension of each RCP from 2100 to 2300, not built with IAMs (p. 15).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Kaya identity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Emissions = population × income per person × energy per unit of income × emissions per unit of energy. Used to see what drives a scenario (p. 17).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Overshoot</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Concentrations rise above the target and are later brought back down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">BECCS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bioenergy with carbon capture and storage, which removes CO2 from the air (’ negative emissions’). RCP2.6 depends on it (p. 17).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"31\" id=\"b31\">The four pathways</h2>\n<table data-b=\"32\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.3.</strong> van Vuuren et al. (2011): The four pathways</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Pathway</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2100 forcing</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Shape (p. 12)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">IAM team</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP2.6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2.6 W/m² (about 490 ppm CO2-eq at peak)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Peaks near 3 W/m² mid-century, then declines</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IMAGE (PBL, Netherlands)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Needs strong policy and negative emissions from bioenergy plus CCS (p. 17)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP4.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4.5 W/m² (about 650 ppm CO2-eq)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stabilises after 2100, no overshoot</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">GCAM (PNNL, USA)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Assumes carbon in natural vegetation is valued, so forests expand (p. 20)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6 W/m² (about 850 ppm CO2-eq)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stabilises after 2100, no overshoot</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">AIM (NIES, Japan)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Heavy fossil reliance, constant carbon factor (p. 18)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP8.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8.5 W/m² (about 1370 ppm CO2-eq)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Keeps rising through 2100</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">MESSAGE (IIASA, Austria)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Based on a revised SRES A2 storyline: high population, lower incomes, slow technology (p. 17)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"33\">How they were chosen (p. 10–11): the team reviewed 324 scenarios from the literature, of which 37 (from 7 modelling teams) met the criteria. They wanted four, an even number so there is no ’ middle’ scenario, spaced about 2 W/m² apart. The set had to cover the literature range, from about 2.5 to 8–9 W/m². RCP8.5 sits near the 90th percentile of no-policy baseline scenarios; RCP4.5 matches the bulk of the scenarios assessed in the IPCC’ s Fourth Assessment Report.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"34\" id=\"b34\">What the paper finds about the pathways (section 3)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"35\"><strong>Energy.</strong> RCP2.6, 4.5 and 6 use 750–900 EJ of primary energy in 2100, about double today. RCP8.5 is far more energy-intensive because of population growth and slow technology (p. 17).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"36\"><strong>Fossil fuels.</strong> Even in the low scenarios, carbon capture means some coal or gas is still used in 2100 at higher levels than in 2000 (p. 17).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"37\"><strong>Land.</strong> RCP8.5 expands cropland and grassland; RCP2.6 expands cropland for bioenergy; RCP4.5 shrinks both through reforestation (p. 19–20).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"38\"><strong>Co-benefits.</strong> Climate policy also cuts air pollutants such as SO2 because both come from burning fossil fuels, especially coal (p. 21). All four RCPs assume air-pollution control tightens as income rises, so they are not suitable for studying worlds where it does not (p. 22).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"39\"><strong>Methane versus nitrous oxide.</strong> Methane breaks down faster, so cuts show up in concentrations sooner; N2O lasts longer, so its concentration rises in every RCP (p. 23).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"40\" id=\"b40\">The limitations section is the most useful part (p. 26–28)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"41\">RCPs are not forecasts, not absolute bounds, and not policy recommendations. No likelihood is attached to any of them.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"42\">The socio-economic stories behind the four RCPs were built independently by four teams, so the set has no common internal logic. Differences between RCPs can come from model differences, not only from climate policy.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"43\">The socio-economic scenario behind each RCP is one of many that could produce the same concentrations.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"44\">Land-use and air-pollution patterns reflect each model’ s own assumptions, not only the forcing level.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"45\">Turning emissions into concentrations and forcing carries its own uncertainty.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"46\">The paper’ s own conclusion is that a socio-economic framework still had to be built to go with the RCPs. That is the next section.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"47\" id=\"b47\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"48\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.4.</strong> van Vuuren et al. (2011): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The word ‘representative’ signifies that each of the RCPs represents a larger set of scenarios in the literature.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining what an RCP is in your scenario chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCPs \"are not the final new, fully integrated scenarios\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stating that RCPs cover climate forcing only, not economics or policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The RCPs should not be interpreted as forecasts or absolute bounds, or be seen as policy prescriptive.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">26</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A caveat whenever you use an RCP-based scenario</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The socio-economic scenarios underlying the RCPs cannot be treated as a set with an overarching internal logic.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">27</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why RCPs alone cannot drive a transition-risk analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Differences between the RCPs, therefore, cannot be directly interpreted as a result of climate policy or particular socio-economic developments.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">27</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Warning against reading policy conclusions off RCP comparisons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCPs span \"2.6 to 8.5 W/m2\" of year-2100 radiative forcing</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">One-line summary of the range</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"49\" id=\"b49\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"50\"><strong>Physical versus transition.</strong> The RCPs describe the physical side: how much warming results from a given amount of gas. They say nothing about how an economy or a bank behaves. Transition risk needs the policy and technology layer that comes with the SSPs and with the scenario sets built on both. (That link to the scenarios used by central banks is my background knowledge, not something this paper says.)</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"51\"><strong>Why low scenarios imply transition risk.</strong> RCP2.6 shows what a low-warming world demands: peak and fast decline in emissions, plus negative emissions. That is the physical logic behind the idea that fossil-fuel assets may be stranded.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"52\"><strong>Reading the labels.</strong> When a report says \"SSP1-2.6\", the 2.6 is this paper’ s forcing level, in W/m².</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"53\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> Mostly yes. If you need the exact harmonization method or the data tables (Tables 1–3), open the paper; for citation and concepts, these notes are enough. Check any quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"54\" id=\"b54\">2. The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"55\"><strong>Sources:</strong> (a) O’ Neill, Kriegler, Riahi, Ebi, Hallegatte, Carter, Mathur, van Vuuren (2014), <em>Climatic Change</em> 122:387–400, \"A new scenario framework for climate change research: the concept of shared socioeconomic pathways\" (your file \"SSP-1.pdf\", 14 pages). (b) O’ Neill, Kriegler, Ebi, Kemp-Benedict, Riahi, Rothman, van Ruijven, van Vuuren, Birkmann, Kok, Levy, Solecki (2017), <em>Global Environmental Change</em> 42:169–180, \"The roads ahead: Narratives for shared socioeconomic pathways describing world futures in the 21st century\" (your file \"SSP-2.pdf\", 12 pages; first published online in 2015). Page numbers are the journals’ own. Ritu Mathur of TERI, New Delhi, is a co-author of the 2014 paper.</p>\n<p data-b=\"56\">The RCPs say how much warming; the SSPs say what kind of society is producing and facing it. An SSP describes a possible world through 2100 in which there is no climate change and no new climate policy, so that researchers can add those later and see their effects separately.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"57\" id=\"b57\">Paper (a), the framework: how to think about the SSPs</h2>\n<p data-b=\"58\"><strong>1. The scenario matrix (p. 388).</strong> Integrated climate scenarios are built from three ingredients:</p>\n<table data-b=\"59\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.5.</strong> The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers): Paper (a), the framework: how to think about the SSPs</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Axis</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What it fixes</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it comes from</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate outcome</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How much radiative forcing and warming</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The four RCPs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Socio-economic development</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Population, income, technology, institutions, in a world without climate policy or climate change</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The SSPs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What climate policy is assumed, to reach a given climate outcome</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Shared climate Policy Assumptions (SPAs)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"60\">The SSPs are only the middle axis. A full scenario needs all three.</p>\n<p data-b=\"61\"><strong>2. SSPs are deliberately unrealistic in one respect (p. 389).</strong> They assume no climate change and no new climate policy. The authors accept that this means they \"may not describe plausible assumptions for the future, but this is an intentional component of the design.\" Reason: if the SSP already contains climate effects, a researcher cannot isolate the effect of climate or of policy.</p>\n<p data-b=\"62\"><strong>3. Pathways versus scenarios (p. 389–390).</strong> A pathway is one component (an RCP or an SSP). A scenario combines pathways with emissions, climate projections and policy to give an integrated picture. Emissions and land use are outputs of models run on the SSP, not part of the SSP.</p>\n<p data-b=\"63\"><strong>4. The challenges space (p. 390–391).</strong> Instead of choosing two causes and building a 2 × 2 of worlds, as the older SRES scenarios did, the authors begin with outcomes. They plot worlds by two kinds of difficulty: how hard it is to mitigate (cut emissions) and how hard it is to adapt. This is called the inverse approach.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"64\"><strong>Challenges to mitigation (p. 391):</strong> high reference emissions without policy (fast population or economic growth, energy-intensive economies, carbon-intensive energy supply), and low mitigative capacity (few technology options, weak institutions, little finance, little political will).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"65\"><strong>Challenges to adaptation (p. 392):</strong> conditions that raise the risk from any given climate change. Risk is the combination of hazard, exposure and vulnerability. The hazard part sits in the RCP and climate models. Exposure, sensitivity and adaptive capacity sit in the SSP. (Compare the IPCC WG2 risk framework later in this layer.)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"66\"><strong>5. Five domains, one pathway each (p. 393).</strong> The challenges space is split into five domains, and one SSP is chosen to represent each. Many pathways could fall in each domain; the SSP is the one picked to be shared across studies.</p>\n<p data-b=\"67\"><strong>6. Basic and extended SSPs (p. 394–397).</strong> Basic SSPs have a narrative and a few quantified drivers (population, GDP, urbanisation). Extended SSPs add sector or regional detail (for example energy, land, agriculture) as needed.</p>\n<p data-b=\"68\"><strong>7. Drivers listed in Table 1 (p. 396):</strong> demographics, economic development, welfare, environment, resources, institutions and governance, technology, broader societal factors, and non-climate policies.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"69\" id=\"b69\">Paper (b), the narratives: what each SSP says</h2>\n<p data-b=\"70\">The 2017 paper gives the stories. They were written by expert judgment through a series of meetings and a public comment round (38 pages of comments from 18 reviewers, p. 172).</p>\n<table data-b=\"71\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.6.</strong> The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers): Paper (b), the narratives: what each SSP says</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Name</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Challenge to mitigation</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Challenge to adaptation</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">In one line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Sustainability: taking the green road</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Inclusive, lower-resource development; low population; rapid shift to renewables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Middle of the road</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Trends continue from history; uneven progress; slow fall in fossil dependence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regional rivalry: a rocky road</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nationalism, weak institutions, slow growth, high inequality, fossil dependence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Inequality: a road divided</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A connected, high-tech elite and a poor, isolated majority; climate action is easy for the few, adaptation hard for the many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fossil-fueled development: taking the highway</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Rapid growth driven by coal, oil and gas; strong human capital; engineered resilience</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"72\"><strong>Points worth knowing (p. 172–175):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"73\"><strong>SSP2 is not a forecast.</strong> The authors say it \"does not imply a simple extrapolation of recent experience\" but a path consistent with historical patterns (p. 172).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"74\"><strong>SSP4 is the odd one out.</strong> It has no match in the older SRES scenarios (2014 paper, Table 2, p. 398) and the authors spend extra space justifying it using the inequality literature (p. 173–174).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"75\"><strong>SSP5 is the high-growth, high-fossil world.</strong> It assumes strong investment in health and education alongside heavy fossil use, and the authors note this combination has \"little historic precedent\" (p. 174).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"76\"><strong>Each SSP is one story among many.</strong> The authors write: \"The storylines presented in this paper are canonical, but the canon is not exclusive\" (section 5, last pages).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"77\"><strong>Open question.</strong> A world could move between domains, for example a technology surprise could shift SSP3 toward SSP4 (section 5).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"78\" id=\"b78\">How RCPs and SSPs combine</h2>\n<p data-b=\"79\">The labels you see in the IPCC reports join the two: SSP1-2.6 means the SSP1 society with a radiative forcing of 2.6 W/m² by 2100. Not every pairing is plausible. A high-forcing world is hard to reach from SSP1, and a very low-forcing world is hard to reach from SSP3 or SSP5. The 2014 paper treats the combination as something to be built with the policy axis (p. 388), not as automatic.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"80\" id=\"b80\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"81\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.7.</strong> The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (two papers): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Paper, page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSPs are \"`reference’ pathways in that they assume no climate change or climate impacts, and no new climate policies\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2014, p. 389</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining the SSPs; explaining why transition risk needs a policy layer added</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"they may not describe plausible assumptions for the future, but this is an intentional component of the design\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2014, p. 389</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defending the use of an SSP baseline without calling it a forecast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the SSPs should be seen as hypothetical development pathways that serve as a starting point for developing integrated scenarios of the future, rather than as plausible scenarios themselves\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2014, p. 399</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Same, in the authors’ conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Socio-economic challenges to adaptation are \"societal or environmental conditions that, by making adaptation more difficult, increase the risks associated with any given projection of climate change\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2014, p. 392</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bridging to the hazard–exposure–vulnerability risk framework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP2 \"does not imply a simple extrapolation of recent experience\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2017, p. 172</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">If you use SSP2 as a middle case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The storylines presented in this paper are canonical, but the canon is not exclusive.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2017, section 5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Noting that SSP results depend on one particular story</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"82\" id=\"b82\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"83\"><strong>Where transition risk lives.</strong> The SSPs deliberately leave climate policy out; policy is a third, separate axis (the SPAs). Transition risk for banks is largely a policy-and-technology question, so an SSP gives you the economic backdrop but not the transition shock itself.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"84\"><strong>India in the framework.</strong> SSP choice matters for exposure: population, income growth, urbanisation and energy demand differ across SSPs, and these set the size of India’ s future energy and credit demand. (Which SSP best fits India is a judgement you will need to argue; the papers here do not choose one for India.)</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"85\"><strong>Mitigation versus adaptation challenge.</strong> The pair maps onto your two risk families: high mitigation challenge, such as SSP3 and SSP5, points to heavier transition risk if policy tightens late; high adaptation challenge, such as SSP3 and SSP4, points to heavier physical risk and weaker repayment capacity.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"86\"><strong>Can you skip the originals?</strong> For the concepts, yes. Open paper (a) if you want the argument for the inverse approach (section 3, p. 390–394) or Table 1 on drivers, and paper (b) if you want the full narratives (the supporting information has longer versions). Check any quote against the original before it goes into the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"87\" id=\"b87\">3. IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"88\"><strong>Source:</strong> IPCC (2023), <em>Climate Change 2023: Synthesis Report</em>, edited by H. Lee and J. Romero. Your folder has two files: \"IPCC_AR6_SYR_FullVolume.pdf\" (186 pages) and \"IPCC_AR6_SYR_LongerReport.pdf\" (81 pages, which is printed pages 35–115 of the full volume). Page numbers below use the report’ s own numbering. \"SPM p. x\" means the Summary for Policymakers (printed pages 1–34 of the full volume); \"Longer Report p. x\" means printed pages 35–115.</p>\n<p data-b=\"89\"><strong>What I read.</strong> I read the whole Summary for Policymakers text, apart from the figures’ fine detail. From the Longer Report I pulled only the passages on stranded assets and finance, found by searching, not by reading the whole section. Where a claim is from the Longer Report, I say so.</p>\n<p data-b=\"90\">The Synthesis Report is the IPCC’ s one-document answer to three questions: what has happened, what happens next under different emission paths, and what must change this decade. It combines the three Working Group reports of the Sixth Assessment Report, 2021–2022 (WG1 physical science, WG2 impacts and adaptation, WG3 mitigation). Its message for your thesis is that the 1.5°C and 2°C paths need deep emission cuts by 2030, and that fossil assets already built or planned do not fit inside them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"91\" id=\"b91\">Vocabulary you need before reading it</h2>\n<table data-b=\"92\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.8.</strong> IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023): Vocabulary you need before reading it</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Calibrated language</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Words like \"likely\" or \"very likely\" and confidence levels (\"high confidence\") are fixed IPCC scales. Ranges in the SPM are very likely ranges (5–95%) unless stated (SPM p. 4, note 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Global warming level (GWL)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A temperature rise above 1850–1900, such as 1.5°C or 2°C. The IPCC compares impacts at a GWL regardless of the year it is reached (Box SPM.1, p. 9).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CO2-eq, GWP100</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Putting different greenhouse gases in one unit, using each gas’ s warming over 100 years (SPM p. 4, note 9).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Carbon budget</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The total CO2 that can still be emitted for a given chance of staying below a temperature. About 0.45°C of warming per 1000 GtCO2 emitted (B.5.2, p. 19).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Net zero CO2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CO2 added to the air equals CO2 removed. Reached earlier than net zero for all greenhouse gases, because some hard-to-abate emissions remain (B.6.2, p. 21).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Carbon dioxide removal (CDR)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Methods that pull CO2 out of the air, such as reforestation or BECCS. Needed to offset residual emissions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Overshoot</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Warming goes above a target level and is later brought back down (B.7, p. 23).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSPx-y</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A scenario from society SSPx with y W/m² of forcing in 2100. For example SSP1-2.6 (footnote 21, p. 9). The SSP forcing tends to be higher than the RCP with the same label (footnote 23, p. 9).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WG3 categories C1–C8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WG3 sorts 1,202 modelled pathways by warming outcome: C1 limits warming to 1.5°C (over 50%) with no or limited overshoot, through to C8 exceeding 4°C (Box SPM.1, Table 1, p. 10).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">NDC</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nationally Determined Contribution: a country’ s own Paris Agreement pledge.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Emissions gap versus implementation gap</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gap between NDCs and the cuts needed for 1.5°C or 2°C, versus the gap between policies actually in place and NDCs (A.4.3 and A.4.4, p. 11).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unabated fossil fuels</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fossil fuels used without capturing most of the CO2, for example 90% or more at a power plant (Longer Report p. 95, note).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lock-in, stranded assets</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Infrastructure built now that keeps emitting for decades; assets that lose value before the end of their expected life because they cannot be used under climate limits.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reasons for Concern</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Five categories used to rate global climate risk: unique and threatened systems, extreme weather, distribution of impacts, global aggregate impacts, large-scale singular events (p. 15, note 38).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"93\" id=\"b93\">The argument in seven steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"94\"><strong>The warming is human-caused and already large.</strong> Global surface temperature was 1.09°C above 1850–1900 in 2011–2020, 1.59°C over land, and rising faster since 1970 than in any 50-year period in at least 2,000 years (A.1.1, p. 4). Net greenhouse gas emissions were 59 ± 6.6 GtCO2-eq in 2019, 54% above 1990 (A.1.4, p. 4).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"95\"><strong>Impacts are already widespread and unequal.</strong> About 3.3 to 3.6 billion people live in contexts highly vulnerable to climate change (A.2.2, p. 5). Economic damages have been detected in agriculture, forestry, fishery, energy and tourism (A.2.6, p. 6).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"96\"><strong>The scenarios.</strong> Box SPM.1 (p. 9) explains the SSP-based scenarios. Best estimates of warming for 2081–2100 are 1.4°C (SSP1-1.9), 1.8°C (SSP1-2.6), 2.7°C (SSP2-4.5), 3.6°C (SSP3-7.0) and 4.4°C (SSP5-8.5) (B.1.1 and note 30, p. 12). Warming is more likely than not to reach 1.5°C in the near term (2021–2040) even under SSP1-1.9.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"97\"><strong>Risk grows with every increment of warming, and is higher than the previous report assessed.</strong> \"Many climate-related risks are higher than assessed in AR5\" (B.2, p. 14). Risks also compound and cascade across sectors and regions (B.2.3, p. 15). For any given warming, risk depends on exposure and vulnerability, which are rising with urbanisation, migration and inequality (B.2.4, p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"98\"><strong>The carbon budget is almost spent.</strong> From the start of 2020 the budget is about 500 GtCO2 for a 50% chance of 1.5°C and 1,150 GtCO2 for a 67% chance of 2°C (B.5.2, p. 19). CO2 from existing fossil infrastructure, without extra abatement, already exceeds the 1.5°C budget (B.5.3, p. 20).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"99\"><strong>The paths that work require cuts now.</strong> Pathways limiting warming to 1.5°C or 2°C involve rapid, deep and mostly immediate cuts this decade, reaching net zero CO2 in the early 2050s and the early 2070s respectively (B.6, p. 20). Table SPM.1 (p. 21) gives the cut from 2019 levels:</p></li>\n</ol>\n<table data-b=\"100\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.9.</strong> IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023): The argument in seven steps</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Cut from 2019 level</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2030</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2035</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2040</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2050</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.5°C (over 50%), all greenhouse gases</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">43%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">60%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">69%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">84%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.5°C (over 50%), CO2 only</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">48%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">65%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">80%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">99%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2°C (over 67%), all greenhouse gases</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">21%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">35%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">46%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">64%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2°C (over 67%), CO2 only</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">22%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">37%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">51%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">73%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"101\"><strong>Current pledges and policies fall short.</strong> Pledges made before COP26 (the November 2021 UN climate conference) would lead to median warming of 2.8°C by 2100 (A.4.3, p. 11); policies actually implemented by end-2020 lead to 3.2°C (A.4.4, p. 11). Delay would \"lock in high-emissions infrastructure, raise risks of stranded assets and cost-escalation\" (C.2, p. 25).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"102\" id=\"b102\">The parts that matter most for a banking thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"103\"><strong>Stranded assets.</strong> In the SPM: delayed action raises \"risks of stranded assets\" (C.2, p. 25; C.2.2, p. 26). In the Longer Report: limiting warming to 2°C or below \"could strand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure\", with a globally discounted value of around USD 1 to 4 trillion from 2015 to 2050 (medium confidence). Early action keeps the stranded amount smaller; delayed action with continued investment in unabated high-emitting infrastructure before 2030 pushes it to the high end (Longer Report p. 95). The Longer Report also says about 80% of coal, 50% of gas and 30% of oil reserves cannot be burned if warming is limited to 2°C (Longer Report p. 58).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"104\"><strong>Cheaper alternatives.</strong> From 2010 to 2019 unit costs fell 85% for solar, 55% for wind and 85% for lithium-ion batteries (A.4.2, p. 11). The report notes that keeping emission-intensive systems may be more expensive than switching in some regions and sectors.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"105\"><strong>Finance flows are the wrong way round.</strong> \"Public and private finance flows for fossil fuels are still greater than those for climate adaptation and mitigation\" (A.4.5, p. 11). Mitigation investment in 1.5°C and 2°C scenarios would need to be three to six times current levels for 2020–2030 (C.7.2, p. 33).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"106\"><strong>The financial sector is named.</strong> There is enough global capital, but barriers to redirecting it. \"Investors, financial intermediaries, central banks and financial regulators can shift the systemic underpricing of climate-related risks\" (C.7.3, p. 33; same wording in the Longer Report p. 112).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"107\"><strong>Carbon pricing alone is weak.</strong> Where used, carbon pricing instruments \"have incentivized low-cost emissions reduction measures but have been less effective, on their own and at prevailing prices\" for higher-cost measures (C.6.4, p. 32).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"108\"><strong>Benefits versus costs.</strong> The global economic and social benefit of limiting warming to 2°C exceeds the cost of mitigation in most of the assessed literature (medium confidence), though cost-benefit analysis is limited in what it can represent (C.2.4, p. 26).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"109\" id=\"b109\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"110\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.10.</strong> IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, Synthesis Report (2023): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"These are quantitative projections and are neither predictions nor forecasts.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 9 (Box SPM.1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Any time you describe scenarios as inputs, not forecasts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Delayed mitigation and adaptation action would lock in high-emissions infrastructure, raise risks of stranded assets and cost-escalation\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 25 (C.2)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Introducing stranded-asset risk with an authoritative source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Projected CO2 emissions from existing fossil fuel infrastructure without additional abatement would exceed the remaining carbon budget for 1.5°C (50%)\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 19 (B.5 summary); also B.5.3, p. 20</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The core physical argument behind transition risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Public and private finance flows for fossil fuels are still greater than those for climate adaptation and mitigation\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 11 (A.4.5)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Showing finance has not yet realigned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Investors, financial intermediaries, central banks and financial regulators can shift the systemic underpricing of climate-related risks\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 33 (C.7.3)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying a role for regulators and capital rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stranded fossil assets \"around USD 1 to 4 trillion from 2015 to 2050\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Longer Report p. 95</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A global magnitude for stranded assets (note: medium confidence)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Global net anthropogenic GHG emissions have been estimated to be 59 ± 6.6 GtCO2-eq in 2019\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 4 (A.1.4)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A baseline emissions figure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Mitigation investment requirements \"a factor of three to six greater than current levels\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 33 (C.7.2)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The scale of the financing gap</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"111\" id=\"b111\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"112\"><strong>A source for the stranded-asset logic.</strong> The cleanest chain is: the carbon budget is small (B.5.2), existing infrastructure already exceeds it (B.5.3), so some assets cannot be used for their full life (Longer Report p. 95). That is the physical reason a transition shock to banks’ loan books is plausible.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"113\"><strong>Timing matters more than level.</strong> The report stresses that delay raises the stranded-asset total and the cost. For a bank, a late and sharp policy response is worse than an early and gradual one. (That bank-level implication is my inference, not an IPCC statement.)</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"114\"><strong>What the IPCC does not give you.</strong> The Synthesis Report has no sector-by-sector loan analysis, no bank risk model, and nothing India-specific on capital adequacy. It is the scientific and scenario backdrop; the banking mechanics come from Layers 3 to 6.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"115\"><strong>India context.</strong> Asia appears among regions projected to face increasing losses and damages (C.2.2, p. 26). For the global net-zero timing that frames India’ s 2070 pledge, see B.6, p. 20 (early 2070s for 2°C).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"116\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the argument and numbers, yes. Still open these four places yourself: Box SPM.1 with Table 1 (p. 9–10), Figure SPM.4 \"burning embers\" (p. 17–18), Table SPM.1 (p. 21), and Longer Report p. 95 for the stranded-asset passage, because you will probably quote them. I did not read the rest of the Longer Report, which gives the evidence behind each SPM statement. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"117\" id=\"b117\">4. IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: Summary for Policymakers (2022)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"118\"><strong>Source:</strong> IPCC (2022), <em>Climate Change 2022: Mitigation of Climate Change</em>, Working Group III contribution to the Sixth Assessment Report, Summary for Policymakers (your file \"IPCC_AR6_WGIII_SummaryForPolicymakers.pdf\", 56 PDF pages; the summary itself is printed pages 3 to about 50). Page numbers below are the summary’ s own, which run four lower than the PDF page.</p>\n<p data-b=\"119\"><strong>What I read.</strong> I read sections B (trends, B.1 to B.7), C.1 to C.4 (pathways, net zero, energy) and E.4 to E.5 (policy and finance). I did not read section D, the other parts of C and E, or the figure panels in detail.</p>\n<p data-b=\"120\">This is the report that answers \"how much must emissions fall, by when, and what happens to fossil assets?\". It is also the source behind the Synthesis Report’ s mitigation numbers, so for your thesis it is the primary citation for stranded assets and for the gap between pledges and the Paris goals.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"121\" id=\"b121\">Terms specific to this report</h2>\n<table data-b=\"122\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.11.</strong> IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: Terms specific to this report</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Immediate action</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate policies adopted between 2020 and at the latest before 2025 to limit warming to a given level (note 26, p. 14). All assessed 1.5°C pathways assume it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C1, C2, C3a</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pathway categories. C1: 1.5°C (over 50%) with no or limited overshoot. C2: back to 1.5°C after high overshoot. C3a: 2°C (over 67%) with immediate action (Table SPM.2).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unconditional and conditional NDC elements</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unconditional: pledges made without conditions. Conditional: pledges that depend on finance, technology transfer or cooperation (note 27, p. 14). India’ s 2031–2035 NDC makes part of its commitments conditional on such support (from the NDC document, covered in the earlier reading plan).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Implementation gap</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gap between emissions from policies actually in place and those implied by NDCs (B.6.1, p. 14).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Emissions gap</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gap between NDC-implied emissions and what 1.5°C or 2°C pathways need (B.6.1, p. 14).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Unabated fossil fuels</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fossil fuel use without capturing most of the CO2 (note 54).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">CCS</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Carbon capture and storage: capture CO2 at a plant or industrial site and store it underground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Illustrative Mitigation Pathways (IMPs)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Five worked examples of routes to the same temperature, for example heavy reliance on net-negative emissions versus high resource efficiency (C.3, p. 24).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"123\" id=\"b123\">What the report finds</h2>\n<p data-b=\"124\"><strong>1. Emissions are still rising, with a slowdown (B.1, p. 6).</strong> Net global greenhouse gas emissions were 59 ± 6.6 GtCO2-eq in 2019, 12% above 2010 and 54% above 1990. The 2010–2019 average was the highest decadal level on record, though growth slowed from 2.1% a year to 1.3% a year. Energy supply produced about 34% of 2019 emissions and industry 24% (B.2.1, p. 8).</p>\n<p data-b=\"125\"><strong>2. The carbon budget is small compared with past emissions (B.1.3, p. 6–7).</strong> Cumulative net CO2 from 1850 to 2019 was 2,400 ± 240 GtCO2. The remaining budget from 2020 is 500 GtCO2 for a 50% chance of 1.5°C and 1,150 GtCO2 for a 67% chance of 2°C. Emissions in 2010–2019 alone equal about four-fifths of the 1.5°C remaining budget.</p>\n<p data-b=\"126\"><strong>3. Clean technology costs have fallen (B.4.1, p. 11).</strong> From 2010 to 2019 unit costs fell 85% for solar, 55% for wind and 85% for lithium-ion batteries. The report notes that large-scale technologies with fewer chances to learn \"have seen minimal cost reductions\".</p>\n<p data-b=\"127\"><strong>4. Pledges are not enough (B.6, p. 14).</strong> NDCs announced before COP26 make it likely that warming will exceed 1.5°C, and holding it below 2°C \"would then rely on a rapid acceleration of mitigation efforts after 2030\". Policies implemented by end-2020 give higher emissions than NDCs. Table SPM.1 (p. 14), in GtCO2-eq per year in 2030:</p>\n<table data-b=\"128\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.12.</strong> IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Measure</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Median emissions under policies implemented by end-2020</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">57 (range 52–60)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Median emissions under NDCs, unconditional elements only</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">53 (range 50–57)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Median emissions under NDCs including conditional elements</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">50 (range 47–55)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Emissions gap to 2°C pathways (immediate action)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10–16 unconditional; 6–14 with conditional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Emissions gap to 1.5°C pathways (immediate action)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">19–26 unconditional; 16–23 with conditional</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"129\"><strong>5. Existing and planned fossil infrastructure already uses up the budget (B.7, p. 16).</strong> Projected cumulative future CO2 from existing fossil infrastructure, if operated as it has been and without extra abatement, is 660 <span>[</span>460–890<span>]</span> GtCO2 from 2018 to the end of its life. Adding planned power-sector infrastructure gives 850 <span>[</span>600–1,100<span>]</span> GtCO2. The comparison:</p>\n<table data-b=\"130\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.13.</strong> IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds (b)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quantity</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">GtCO2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Future CO2 from existing fossil infrastructure</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">660</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Same, plus planned power-sector infrastructure</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">850</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cumulative net CO2 to net zero in 1.5°C pathways</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">510</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cumulative net CO2 to net zero in 2°C pathways</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">890</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"131\">So existing infrastructure alone exceeds the 1.5°C path (B.7), and existing plus planned infrastructure is roughly equal to the 2°C path. This is the quantitative core of the stranded-asset argument.</p>\n<p data-b=\"132\"><strong>6. Emissions must peak and then fall fast (C.1, p. 17).</strong> Emissions peak between 2020 and at the latest before 2025 in 1.5°C pathways, and in 2°C pathways with immediate action. Without stronger policy, the median warming is 3.2 <span>[</span>2.2 to 3.5<span>]</span>°C by 2100. Required cuts from 2019 (C.1.1, p. 17):</p>\n<table data-b=\"133\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.14.</strong> IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds (c)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Pathway</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">By 2030</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">By 2050</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.5°C (C1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">43% <span>[</span>34–60<span>]</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">84% <span>[</span>73–98<span>]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2°C with immediate action (C3a)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">27% <span>[</span>13–45<span>]</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">63% <span>[</span>52–76<span>]</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Return to 1.5°C after high overshoot (C2)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">23% <span>[</span>0–44<span>]</span></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">75% <span>[</span>62–91<span>]</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"134\">Note on numbers: the Synthesis Report’ s Table SPM.1 gives 21% for 2030 for 2°C because it covers the wider C3 category; the 27% above is for C3a (immediate action). Quote the one that matches the category you mean.</p>\n<p data-b=\"135\"><strong>7. Net zero dates (C.2, p. 23).</strong> Net zero CO2 comes in the early 2050s in 1.5°C pathways and around the early 2070s in 2°C pathways, with cumulative net CO2 until net zero of 510 GtCO2 and 890 GtCO2 respectively (C.2.1).</p>\n<p data-b=\"136\"><strong>8. Fossil fuel use must fall steeply by 2050 (C.3.2, p. 24).</strong> Median decline in global use relative to 2019:</p>\n<table data-b=\"137\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.15.</strong> IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: What the report finds (d)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Fuel</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">1.5°C pathways</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">2°C pathways</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Coal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">about 95%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">85%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Oil</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">about 60%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gas</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">about 45%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">15%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"138\">In 2050 almost all electricity comes from zero- or low-carbon sources.</p>\n<p data-b=\"139\"><strong>9. Stranded assets, with timing (C.4.4, p. 28–29).</strong> \"Limiting global warming to 2°C or below will leave a substantial amount of fossil fuels unburned and could strand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure.\" The combined discounted value of unburned fossil fuels and stranded infrastructure is projected at around USD 1–4 trillion from 2015 to 2050 for about 2°C, and higher for 1.5°C (medium confidence). Coal assets are at risk of being stranded before 2030; oil and gas assets nearer mid-century. CCS, if available, could allow fossil fuels to be used longer.</p>\n<p data-b=\"140\"><strong>10. Policy and finance (E.4 to E.5, p. 46–47).</strong> Carbon pricing alone, at prevailing prices, was not enough for higher-cost measures. Investment needs for 2020–2030 in 2°C or 1.5°C scenarios are three to six times current levels. There is enough global capital, but barriers stop it moving, including \"inadequate assessment of climate-related risks and investment opportunities\" (E.5.2).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"141\" id=\"b141\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"142\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.16.</strong> IPCC Working Group 3, Mitigation of Climate Change: Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Limiting global warming to 2°C or below will leave a substantial amount of fossil fuels unburned and could strand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C.4.4, p. 28–29</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining stranded-asset risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"coal assets are projected to be at risk of being stranded before 2030, while oil and gas assets are projected to be more at risk of being stranded towards mid-century\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C.4.4, p. 28–29</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Timing of transition risk; relevant for coal-heavy exposures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Existing and planned fossil infrastructure \"exceed the total cumulative net CO2 emissions in pathways that limit warming to 1.5°C\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">B.7, p. 16</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The carbon-budget argument in one sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Likely limiting warming to below 2°C would then rely on a rapid acceleration of mitigation efforts after 2030.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">B.6, p. 14</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why a late, sharp policy shift is a plausible scenario</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Maintaining emission-intensive systems may, in some regions and sectors, be more expensive than transitioning to low emission systems.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C.4.2, p. 28</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Counter-argument to the idea that transition is always costly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"inadequate assessment of climate-related risks and investment opportunities\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">E.5.2, p. 47</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motivating better risk assessment inside financial institutions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Net zero CO2 \"in the early 2050s\" (1.5°C) and \"around the early 2070s\" (2°C)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C.2, p. 23</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Global timing context for India’ s 2070 pledge</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"143\" id=\"b143\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"144\"><strong>The strongest single source for the stranded-asset case.</strong> Use B.7 for the quantity (660 to 850 GtCO2 against budgets of 510 and 890) and C.4.4 for the value (USD 1–4 trillion) and the timing (coal first).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"145\"><strong>Scenario design.</strong> The 2030 emission levels and the cut percentages in C.1.1 give you numbers to anchor an orderly versus delayed transition story. (The orderly/disorderly labels are a central-bank scenario convention, not IPCC wording.)</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"146\"><strong>Policy uncertainty is part of the picture.</strong> The gap between implemented policies (57 GtCO2-eq) and NDCs (50 to 53) means policy outcomes are themselves uncertain, which feeds directly into how much transition risk a bank faces.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"147\"><strong>India.</strong> The report is global and does not give a bank-by-bank or India-specific stranded-asset estimate. India’ s 2070 net zero sits at the 2°C-pathway date (early 2070s) in C.2. India’ s own conditionality of NDC elements comes from its NDC document, not from this report.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"148\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the numbers above, yes. Open the document itself for Table SPM.2 (the full list of pathway categories with temperatures and net zero dates), B.7 (p. 16) and C.4.4 (p. 28–29), because you will probably quote them. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"149\" id=\"b149\">5. IPCC Working Group 2, Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability (2022)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"150\"><strong>Source:</strong> IPCC (2022), <em>Climate Change 2022: Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability</em>, Working Group II contribution to the Sixth Assessment Report (your file \"IPCC_AR6_WGII_FullReport.pdf\", 3,068 PDF pages, about 380 MB). Page references: \"SPM p. x\" is the report’ s own Summary for Policymakers numbering (PDF page minus 12); \"Ch. 10 p. x\" and \"Ch. 16 p. x\" use the report’ s running page numbers.</p>\n<p data-b=\"151\"><strong>What I read.</strong> I read the Summary for Policymakers sections A and B (introduction, observed impacts, vulnerability, near-term and long-term risks, compound risks, overshoot) in full. I read the executive summaries of Chapter 10 (Asia) and Chapter 16 (Key Risks across Sectors and Regions). I searched the rest of the report for banking and finance terms. I did not read the other 3,000 pages, nor section C onward of the summary (adaptation and resilient development).</p>\n<p data-b=\"152\">This is the report that supplies the risk framework your thesis will lean on: risk is hazard, exposure and vulnerability together, and each can change independently. That is why the same physical hazard can mean very different losses for different borrowers.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"153\" id=\"b153\">The framework in the IPCC’ s own terms</h2>\n<table data-b=\"154\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.17.</strong> IPCC Working Group 2, Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability (2022): The framework in the IPCC’ s own terms</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Definition (paraphrased from the SPM notes)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The potential for adverse consequences for human or ecological systems (note 5, p. 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hazard</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A natural or human-induced physical event or trend that may cause loss, injury or damage. WG1 measures the physical side as \"climatic impact-drivers\" (note 6, p. 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Exposure</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The presence of people, assets, infrastructure, ecosystems or livelihoods in places that could be adversely affected (note 7, p. 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Vulnerability</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The propensity or predisposition to be adversely affected; includes sensitivity and lack of capacity to cope and adapt (note 8, p. 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Key risk</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A risk with potentially severe consequences from hazards interacting with the vulnerability of exposed systems (note 9, p. 5). The report identifies 127 of them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Adaptation limits</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hard limit: no adaptive action can avoid intolerable risk. Soft limit: options exist but are not currently available (note 11, p. 7).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Maladaptation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Actions that increase risk of adverse climate outcomes, including through more emissions or shifted vulnerability (note 15, p. 7).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reasons for Concern</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Five categories of global risk, used in the \"burning embers\" diagram (p. 13 and Figure SPM.3b).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Overshoot</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Warming exceeds 1.5°C and returns to or below it later (note 27, p. 8).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Losses and damages</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Observed impacts or projected risks, economic and non-economic (note 19, p. 7).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"155\">Figure SPM.1 (p. 6) draws this as a \"risk propeller\": risk emerges from the overlap of climate hazard, exposure and vulnerability. Human responses can themselves add risk. The Introduction (p. 5) says so directly: \"The risk that can be introduced by human responses to climate change is a new aspect considered in the risk concept.\"</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"156\" id=\"b156\">What the summary finds</h2>\n<p data-b=\"157\"><strong>1. Impacts are already widespread, and unequal (B.1 and B.2, p. 9 and 12).</strong> Adverse impacts attributed to human-induced climate change are widespread and pervasive. About 3.3 to 3.6 billion people live in contexts highly vulnerable to climate change. Human vulnerability hotspots include South Asia. Between 2010 and 2020, mortality from floods, droughts and storms was 15 times higher in highly vulnerable regions than in regions with very low vulnerability (B.2.4, p. 12).</p>\n<p data-b=\"158\"><strong>2. Vulnerability and exposure are partly driven by development choices (B.2, p. 12).</strong> Unsustainable development patterns raise exposure. Future exposure increases with migration, inequality and urbanisation (B.2.5, p. 12–13). Key infrastructure (water, transport, energy, communications) becomes more vulnerable if design standards ignore a changing climate.</p>\n<p data-b=\"159\"><strong>3. In the near term, who and what is exposed matters more than the emissions scenario (B.3.2, p. 13).</strong> Risk to systems \"depend more strongly on changes in their vulnerability and exposure than on differences in climate hazards between emissions scenarios.\" Many near-term risks are unavoidable whichever scenario happens.</p>\n<p data-b=\"160\"><strong>4. Risk thresholds are lower than previously thought (B.3.3, p. 13).</strong> Very high risk emerges in all five Reasons for Concern between 1.2°C and 4.5°C of warming, against two of five in the previous assessment. Risk to unique and threatened systems moves from high to very high at a median of 1.5°C, and risk from extreme weather at a median of 2.0°C.</p>\n<p data-b=\"161\"><strong>5. Mid- to long-term impacts grow with every increment (B.4, p. 14).</strong> For the 127 key risks, assessed impacts are up to multiple times higher than currently observed. Flood damages without adaptation are 1.4 to 2 times higher at 2°C and 2.5 to 3.9 times higher at 3°C than at 1.5°C (B.4.2, p. 14).</p>\n<p data-b=\"162\"><strong>6. Assets at risk (B.4.5, p. 15).</strong> By 2100 the value of global assets within the future 1-in-100-year coastal floodplains is projected to be US$7.9 to US$12.7 trillion (2011 value) under RCP4.5, and US$8.8 to US$14.2 trillion under RCP8.5 (medium confidence).</p>\n<p data-b=\"163\"><strong>7. Economic damages are uncertain and rise non-linearly (B.4.6, p. 15).</strong> The report says the range of global damage estimates, and the lack of comparability between methods, \"does not allow for identification of a robust range of estimates\". Damages per person are often larger as a share of income in developing countries.</p>\n<p data-b=\"164\"><strong>8. Risks compound and cascade (B.5, p. 18–19).</strong> Simultaneous hazards and interacting risks can compound overall risk. Impacts also cross borders through supply chains, markets and natural resource flows (B.5.3, p. 19).</p>\n<p data-b=\"165\"><strong>9. Overshoot is costly (B.6, p. 19).</strong> If warming transiently exceeds 1.5°C, many systems face additional severe risks and some impacts are irreversible even after warming falls again.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"166\" id=\"b166\">Asia and India (Chapter 10 executive summary, p. 1459–1461)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"167\"><strong>Energy and industry.</strong> \"Among 13 developing countries with large energy consumption in Asia, 11 are exposed to high-energy insecurity and industrial-systems risk\" (p. 1459).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"168\"><strong>Water.</strong> By mid-century the transboundary basins of the Amu Darya, Indus and Ganges could face severe water scarcity (p. 1459).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"169\"><strong>Agriculture.</strong> In India, rice production can decrease by 10 to 30% and maize by 25 to 70% for a temperature rise of 1°C to 4°C (p. 1460).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"170\"><strong>Cities.</strong> Asia has the largest share of people living in informal settlements (197 million in Central and Southern Asia). Coastal cities in South and Southeast Asia face large increases in average annual economic losses from flooding between 2005 and 2050. By 2080, 940 million to 1.1 billion urban dwellers in South and Southeast Asia could be affected by extreme heat lasting more than 30 days a year (p. 1460).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"171\"><strong>Barriers to resilience.</strong> The chapter lists fragmented, reactive governance, inadequate evidence on what to prioritise, and finance deficits (p. 1461).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"172\" id=\"b172\">Economics (Chapter 16 executive summary, p. 2414)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"173\">Extreme weather events \"not only cause substantial direct economic damage\" but also reduce economic growth in the short term and, with medium confidence, in the long term, up to 15 years after the event, with more severe impacts in developing than in industrialised economies.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"174\" id=\"b174\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"175\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.18.</strong> IPCC Working Group 2, Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability (2022): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Risk \"can arise from the dynamic interactions among climate-related hazards\", \"the exposure and vulnerability of affected human and ecological systems\" (footnote markers omitted)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining physical risk with the IPCC framework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The risk that can be introduced by human responses to climate change is a new aspect considered in the risk concept.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying treating transition (response) risk as part of climate risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Near-term risks \"depend more strongly on changes in their vulnerability and exposure than on differences in climate hazards between emissions scenarios\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 13 (B.3.2)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arguing that exposure data matter more than scenario choice for near-term bank risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Multiple climate hazards will occur simultaneously, and multiple climatic and non-climatic risks will interact, resulting in compounding overall risk and risks cascading across sectors and regions.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 18 (B.5)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Motivating interaction and contagion effects beyond a single-factor model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Weather and climate extremes are causing economic and societal impacts across national boundaries through supply-chains, markets, and natural resource flows\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 19 (B.5.3)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cross-border and supply-chain channels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The range of global damage estimates and \"lack of comparability between methodologies\" \"does not allow for identification of a robust range of estimates\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SPM p. 15 (B.4.6)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Acknowledging uncertainty in damage functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"In India, rice production can decrease from 10 to 30%, whereas maize production can decrease from 25 to 70%\" for 1°C to 4°C</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Ch. 10, p. 1460</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Agricultural credit exposure in India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Extreme weather events reduce economic growth \"in the short term\" and \"in the long term (up to 15 years after the event)\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Ch. 16, p. 2414</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Linking physical shocks to borrowers’ repayment capacity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"176\" id=\"b176\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"177\"><strong>A ready physical-risk framework.</strong> Hazard, exposure and vulnerability give you a clean way to separate what the climate does from what a bank’ s borrowers are exposed to. The IPCC treats exposure and vulnerability as changing independently over time, so a loan book’ s physical risk can change without any change in the climate.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"178\"><strong>Transition risk has IPCC cover.</strong> The \"risk from human responses\" wording (p. 5) lets you present transition risk as part of the same risk concept, rather than as a separate idea (my reading of how the passage can be used).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"179\"><strong>Where this report is thin.</strong> WG2 has no bank or capital-adequacy analysis, and the search of the full report found few passages on the financial sector. The finance mechanics come from Layers 3 to 6. India appears through the Asia chapter, mainly for agriculture, water, energy and cities.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"180\"><strong>Caution on numbers.</strong> Many WG2 results are \"without additional adaptation\" or medium confidence. Quote the confidence level with the figure.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"181\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For concepts and headline findings, yes. Open these yourself: Figure SPM.1 (p. 6, the risk propeller), Figure SPM.3 (p. 16–17, global and regional risks and the \"burning embers\"), and B.3.2 (p. 13) if you plan to quote it. I did not read Chapter 10 or 16 beyond their executive summaries, so for anything India-specific go to the body of Chapter 10. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"182\" id=\"b182\">6. Master quotables list and how Layer 1 feeds the thesis</h1>\n<p data-b=\"183\">The ten quotes below are the ones I would reach for first. Each is repeated from its section above with the same page reference; the full tables in sections 1 to 5 hold more. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"184\" id=\"b184\">Top quotables, by job</h2>\n<table data-b=\"185\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.19.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 1 feeds the thesis: Top quotables, by job</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Job in the thesis</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source and page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Scenarios are not forecasts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"These are quantitative projections and are neither predictions nor forecasts.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">AR6 SYR SPM p. 9 (Box SPM.1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What an RCP is</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The word ‘representative’ signifies that each of the RCPs represents a larger set of scenarios in the literature.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP paper p. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Caveat on RCP comparisons</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The RCPs should not be interpreted as forecasts or absolute bounds, or be seen as policy prescriptive.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">RCP paper p. 26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why SSPs need a policy layer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSPs are \"`reference’ pathways in that they assume no climate change or climate impacts, and no new climate policies\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP paper (2014) p. 389</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defending the SSP baseline</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"they may not describe plausible assumptions for the future, but this is an intentional component of the design\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SSP paper (2014) p. 389</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stranded-asset risk, defined</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Limiting global warming to 2°C or below will leave a substantial amount of fossil fuels unburned and could strand considerable fossil fuel infrastructure\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WG3 SPM C.4.4, p. 28–29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Timing of stranding</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"coal assets are projected to be at risk of being stranded before 2030, while oil and gas assets are projected to be more at risk of being stranded towards mid-century\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WG3 SPM C.4.4, p. 28–29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The carbon-budget argument</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Existing and planned fossil infrastructure \"exceed the total cumulative net CO2 emissions in pathways that limit warming to 1.5°C\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WG3 SPM B.7, p. 16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Role of financial regulators</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Investors, financial intermediaries, central banks and financial regulators can shift the systemic underpricing of climate-related risks\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">AR6 SYR SPM p. 33 (C.7.3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Physical risk is exposure and vulnerability too</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Near-term risks \"depend more strongly on changes in their vulnerability and exposure than on differences in climate hazards between emissions scenarios\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">WG2 SPM p. 13 (B.3.2)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"186\" id=\"b186\">How the five sources fit together</h2>\n<table data-b=\"187\">\n<caption><strong>Table 2.20.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 1 feeds the thesis: How the five sources fit together</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Thesis question</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where to look</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What you get</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How much warming for a given emissions path?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Section 1 (RCP paper)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The physical side only: forcing levels 2.6 to 8.5 W/m².</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What kind of world are we in?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Section 2 (SSP papers)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Five development pathways, with no climate policy built in.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How do scenario labels and global numbers line up?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Section 3 (Synthesis Report)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Warming levels, carbon budgets, net zero dates, the 1–4 trillion stranded-asset range.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">How big is the transition and which assets are hit?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Section 4 (WG3 SPM)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Required cuts, fossil fuel decline, stranded-asset timing, policy and finance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">What does physical risk look like, and for India?</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Section 5 (WG2)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hazard, exposure and vulnerability; Asia and India findings; compounding risk.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"188\" id=\"b188\">Four ideas to carry out of Layer 1</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"189\"><strong>Physical and transition risk need different sources.</strong> The RCPs and WG2 describe what the climate does and what it hits. The transition shock comes from policy and technology, which the SSPs deliberately leave out (SSP paper p. 389) and which WG3 describes.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"190\"><strong>Scenarios are inputs, not predictions.</strong> Say so with the Box SPM.1 quote, and say which scenario you use and why.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"191\"><strong>Timing matters as much as the level.</strong> Late and sharp action raises stranded-asset losses and costs (SYR C.2; WG3 B.6). The orderly versus disorderly labels are a central-bank convention, not IPCC wording.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"192\"><strong>None of these documents analyses banks.</strong> There is no capital-adequacy, loan-book or Indian public sector bank analysis in Layer 1. It gives the scientific and scenario backdrop; the banking mechanics come from Layers 3 to 6. India enters here only through the Asia chapter of WG2 and the 2070 net zero date (SYR B.6; WG3 C.2).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"193\" id=\"b193\">What I did not read</h2>\n<p data-b=\"194\">The body of the Synthesis Report Longer Report beyond p. 95, the body of the WG3 report, the WG2 report beyond the Summary for Policymakers and the executive summaries of Chapters 10 and 16, and WG2 section C onward. If your thesis leans on one of those, tell me and I will read it next.</p>\n","s":[[23,"b4"],[24,"b20"],[27,"b54"],[31,"b87"],[35,"b117"],[39,"b149"],[42,"b182"]]};